WEBVTT

1
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:09,400
<i>I remember exactly where I was.</i>

2
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,560
Freedom!

3
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,040
<i>Only in moments of great upheaval,</i>

4
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:19,120
<i>in very serious events,</i>
<i>can we say that phrase.</i>

5
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:21,760
<i>"I remember it perfectly."</i>

6
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:26,160
I wish this were the last time
we had to take to the streets.

7
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:28,920
They need to stop. Enough is enough.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,920
This isn't right.
We're ashamed to say we're Basque!

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,520
We have this pain inside us that…

10
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:37,440
I can barely speak.

11
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:42,280
ETA has kidnapped someone again.

12
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:46,800
They are threatening
to kill the hostage in 48 hours.

13
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:49,160
{\an8}The victim is Miguel Ángel Blanco Garrido.

14
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:52,760
<i>The National Police and the Civil Guard</i>
<i>set up a command center…</i>

15
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:56,520
<i>Once again, we are shaken by news</i>
<i>of a kidnapping. Miguel Ángel Blanco…</i>

16
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,240
<i>The terrorist group ETA,</i>
<i>as you already know,</i>

17
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:01,600
<i>has issued an all-or-nothing challenge</i>
<i>to the state</i>

18
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:05,560
<i>with a kidnapping,</i>
<i>a death threat, and a specific deadline…</i>

19
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,040
<i>In the summer of 1997,</i>

20
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,400
<i>one of those key events took place</i>
<i>that changed everything.</i>

21
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:20,640
<i>The TV networks led their news programs</i>
<i>with another attack</i>

22
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:22,800
<i>by the terrorist organization ETA.</i>

23
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:24,320
<i>But this time…</i>

24
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:26,360
<i>This time was different.</i>

25
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:32,920
<i>Because you remember</i>
<i>exactly where you were</i>

26
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,200
<i>when you heard about Miguel Ángel Blanco,</i>
<i>don't you?</i>

27
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:39,800
Oh, God…

28
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:45,760
Honestly, when I think about it,

29
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:52,200
{\an8}I feel the same sadness
and the same outrage, you know?

30
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:54,440
{\an8}HIS MAJESTY THE KING

31
00:01:55,520 --> 00:02:00,600
{\an8}We all remember
almost every detail of where we were,

32
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:02,720
{\an8}what we were doing, how we felt…

33
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:06,200
I remember my mother telling me

34
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:09,240
that Miguel Ángel left home a little early

35
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:11,600
{\an8}because he had a meeting at 4:00 p.m.…

36
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:12,680
{\an8}MIGUEL ÁNGEL'S SISTER

37
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:14,440
{\an8}…and wanted to be there on time.

38
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:21,920
We called him "Miguel."
Miguel Ángel, well,

39
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,600
{\an8}that came later…
But to us, he was "Miguel."

40
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:25,520
{\an8}COWORKER

41
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,240
I remember it
like it was just a few days ago.

42
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:32,600
You can't forget that Thursday, July 10th.

43
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:36,480
So, at 3:30 p.m.…
Well, everyone was usually punctual,

44
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:38,680
and Miguel always arrived on time.

45
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:40,760
It was a bit strange.

46
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:41,960
At 3:40 p.m., we were saying,

47
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:44,840
"Miguel hasn't arrived.
He must've gotten held up somewhere."

48
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:50,080
He said goodbye like he did every day and,
suddenly, at 4:10 p.m.,

49
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:53,240
Eman Consulting called
asking for my brother,

50
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,600
because he hadn't come in.

51
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:57,360
My mother was surprised. She said,

52
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:00,360
"That's strange.
He even left ten minutes early

53
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:03,160
in case anything came up on the way."

54
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:05,640
It seemed a bit odd to us.
He still hadn't shown up.

55
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:13,080
At 4:30 p.m., they called her again
to say he still hadn't shown up.

56
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:18,800
And my mother told me
she immediately feared the worst.

57
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:23,560
So she called Mendaro Hospital,

58
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:25,680
the closest one to Ermua,

59
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:29,360
to see if anyone named Miguel Ángel Blanco
had been admitted.

60
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:33,200
And they confirmed
they hadn't admitted anyone by that name.

61
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:39,200
My colleagues in Hernani called to say
Egin Irratia had received a call

62
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,920
{\an8}from someone speaking on behalf of ETA…

63
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,160
{\an8}JOURNALIST AT EGIN NEWSPAPER

64
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:46,240
{\an8}…saying they had kidnapped a councilman,

65
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:50,920
and would kill him
if he wasn't released within 48 hours.

66
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,640
So I got in touch
with the Ministry of the Interior.

67
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:57,080
ETA BOMBS DETONATE, NO DAMAGE

68
00:03:57,160 --> 00:04:01,040
I got to my office,
and people were already interrupting me

69
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:02,520
{\an8}to tell me…

70
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:03,640
{\an8}FORMER INTERIOR MINISTER

71
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:05,400
{\an8}They handed me a note saying

72
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,840
there seemed to have been a kidnapping.

73
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:10,760
{\an8}So I met up with Manu and Fernando.

74
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:12,080
{\an8}MIGUEL'S FRIEND

75
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:14,200
{\an8}Manu, the guitarist.
Fernando, the pianist.

76
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:15,520
Then Manu…

77
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:19,560
at that time,

78
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:23,840
found out what had happened
because he heard it on the radio.

79
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:27,800
And we ran to Miguel's house.

80
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:31,240
I was living in a residence hall
in London at the time.

81
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:33,440
I was supposed to stay there
until September.

82
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:38,120
And I got a call
from a friend at the residence hall.

83
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:44,000
They put the call through
and said, "Call home."

84
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:46,480
I said, "I'm going out,
I'll call when I get back."

85
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:48,520
And he said, "No. Call now. It's urgent."

86
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:54,480
I was in the Hall of Columns
at Moncloa Palace.

87
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:56,080
{\an8}My aide handed me a note.

88
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:57,960
{\an8}FORMER PRIME MINISTER

89
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:00,360
{\an8}I opened it and read:

90
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:05,360
"A Partido Popular councilman
has been kidnapped in Ermua."

91
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:12,080
Consuelo, his mother, was there,
along with Marimar Díaz, his girlfriend,

92
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:13,960
and Ramón Jáuregui,

93
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:17,240
then secretary general
of the Euskadi Socialist Party.

94
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:21,800
And at one point,
Ramón Jáuregui took us aside.

95
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:24,880
I had never spoken to him before.

96
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:30,440
And he said, "Give up hope.
They're going to kill your friend."

97
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:10,360
{\an8}MIGUEL ÁNGEL BLANCO:
THE 48 HOURS THAT CHANGED SPAIN

98
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:16,760
{\an8}NARRATED BY JON SISTIAGA

99
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:31,760
<i>Happy 1997!</i>

100
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:34,360
<i>This is Spain in 1997,</i>

101
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:38,080
<i>the year the Guggenheim opened</i>
<i>and Princess Cristina got married.</i>

102
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:39,320
I do.

103
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:42,440
<i>And Prime Minister José María Aznar's</i>
<i>"Spain is doing well."</i>

104
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:45,320
<i>That Spain is nothing like today's.</i>

105
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:48,240
<i>Only a few people had a cell phone,</i>

106
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:52,320
<i>homes had no Wi-Fi,</i>
<i>and social media had not been invented.</i>

107
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:56,920
<i>Many of us were devastated because</i>
<i>five-time Tour winner Miguel Induráin</i>

108
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,080
{\an8}<i>announced his retirement.</i>

109
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:01,640
<i>It was summer,</i>
<i>and at the beach bars we were split</i>

110
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:04,000
<i>between Alejandro Sanz's "Corazón Partío"</i>

111
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:06,160
<i>and Calamaro's "La Flaca."</i>

112
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:12,040
<i>By that point in the year,</i>
<i>ETA had already killed nine people.</i>

113
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:16,000
<i>In fact, over the previous 30 years,</i>
<i>it had murdered almost 800 people</i>

114
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:18,440
<i>in the name of Basque independence.</i>

115
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:21,640
<i>Day in and day out,</i>
<i>newspapers and TV networks led</i>

116
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:24,640
<i>with ETA attacks or arrests of terrorists.</i>

117
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:29,240
<i>Six hundred ETA members were</i>
<i>in Spanish and French prisons that year.</i>

118
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:33,440
{\an8}<i>I was 29,</i>
<i>the same age as Miguel Ángel Blanco,</i>

119
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,640
<i>and I had already covered</i>
<i>my first wars as a journalist.</i>

120
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:38,880
<i>But I came back home, to Euskadi,</i>

121
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:43,280
<i>and I was still talking about death,</i>
<i>darkness, shock, and blood.</i>

122
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:44,280
THE PEOPLE, ALIVE

123
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:47,320
<i>A language that,</i>
<i>as a Basque, felt familiar to me.</i>

124
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:03,600
<i>ETA was asking for something impossible,</i>
<i>and it knew it.</i>

125
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:05,680
<i>Something cruel and twisted.</i>

126
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:08,760
<i>If the government</i>
<i>did not move ETA prisoners</i>

127
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:10,600
<i>to prisons in Euskadi within two days,</i>

128
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:13,800
<i>they'd kill</i>
<i>the young Partido Popular councilman.</i>

129
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:14,840
ENOUGH!

130
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:16,280
<i>All that was left was to wait.</i>

131
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:19,440
<i>All we could hope for was mercy.</i>

132
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:28,000
<i>The 48 hours</i>
<i>that would change everything had begun.</i>

133
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:29,920
THURSDAY
4:00 P.M.

134
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,160
48 HOURS LEFT

135
00:08:31,240 --> 00:08:34,840
<i>We interrupt our programming to inform you</i>
<i>that there is strong evidence</i>

136
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:37,880
<i>of a possible kidnapping</i>
<i>by the terrorist group ETA.</i>

137
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:42,160
It was just another afternoon
in a tiny, two-person newsroom.

138
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:43,400
{\an8}And we used to…

139
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:44,360
{\an8}JOURNALIST

140
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:48,560
{\an8}One of our most important sources
was the scanner,

141
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:52,760
a device we bought abroad
that picked up police frequencies.

142
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:58,520
That afternoon,
I clearly remember a lot of commotion.

143
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,200
Police chatter was coming through,

144
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:03,920
talking about the Eibar area, Ermua…
I said, "Something strange is going on."

145
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:10,400
Judge García-Castellón
is on duty at the National Court.

146
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:11,800
He has opened…

147
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:15,640
We were notified
that a Partido Popular councilman

148
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:17,680
had been kidnapped in the Basque Country,

149
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:21,280
{\an8}and that it looked
like blackmail against the state.

150
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:22,760
{\an8}FORMER NATIONAL COURT JUDGE

151
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:28,720
A military plane immediately
flew me to Sondika Airport, in Bilbao.

152
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:31,240
{\an8}We were on our way back to Donosti.

153
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:32,720
{\an8}CIVIL GUARD LIEUTENANT COLONEL

154
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:36,160
{\an8}We had stopped
at a service area for coffee,

155
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:41,280
and news started coming in there,
still nothing definite,

156
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:43,880
but clearly something was happening.

157
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:50,680
I remember our news director telling me…

158
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:51,720
{\an8}JOURNALIST

159
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:56,720
{\an8}…"Grab some clothes and get moving.
They've given this kid 48 hours."

160
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:59,200
And I think I came to Bilbao, right?

161
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:00,600
Yes, we had a chat,

162
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:04,160
and then you joined us around midday.

163
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:08,040
And we did another reconstruction
using the footage,

164
00:10:08,120 --> 00:10:11,360
and started planning the next few hours.

165
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:14,320
{\an8}The first report we saw
on Televisión Española said

166
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:17,720
{\an8}that a young PP councilman from Eibar
had been kidnapped.

167
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:19,840
We all looked at each other

168
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:22,400
because I was
the young PP councilman from Eibar.

169
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:25,800
{\an8}I found out
about Miguel Ángel's kidnapping

170
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:30,360
{\an8}because a former university classmate
walked by and said,

171
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:34,400
"God, Luis, what a scare.
I thought they'd kidnapped you."

172
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:36,600
I said, "Kidnapped me?"

173
00:10:37,280 --> 00:10:39,600
"Yes, I just heard on the radio

174
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:42,000
that a councilman from Ermua
has been kidnapped."

175
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:48,600
I called my mother and said,

176
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:52,280
"Mom, I don't know if you've
heard the news, but it's not me."

177
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:56,440
She asked, "What happened?"
"They've kidnapped a young PP councilman."

178
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:58,560
"They're saying he's from Eibar,
but it's not me."

179
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:00,280
An Ertzaintza unit arrived

180
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:04,040
and stayed with the family
until the very end.

181
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:06,720
They took over the house
and the phone and said,

182
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:11,640
"We advise you not to watch TV.
We'll answer the phone."

183
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:16,760
I called home
and an unfamiliar voice answered.

184
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:19,400
They said, "Who is this?"
And I said, "Who are <i>you</i>?"

185
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,960
Of course, I didn't recognize the voice.

186
00:11:22,040 --> 00:11:24,720
"I'm Consuelo and Miguel's daughter."

187
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:27,360
And they said,
"Hold on, I'll put your mom on."

188
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:32,560
Then my mother came on and, crying,
said, "ETA has kidnapped your brother."

189
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:37,840
From that moment on,
everyone knew what to do:

190
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:43,840
get back in the car
and rush off to Donosti,

191
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:46,000
take off our suits,

192
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:51,120
change into our protest clothes,
and take to the streets.

193
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:53,400
The fact no one saw anything…

194
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:56,000
Across the street,
if I remember correctly, there were cafés.

195
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:01,320
On a Thursday at that time,
people would be having coffee or lunch.

196
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:07,280
I will never understand
how no one saw anything at all.

197
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:09,200
Not a single gesture… Nothing.

198
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:16,320
<i>Why did they choose him?</i>
<i>Because he was an easy target?</i>

199
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:18,120
<i>A trusting young man</i>

200
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:22,400
<i>who took the same train from Ermua</i>
<i>to Eibar at the same time every day,</i>

201
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:25,240
<i>and worked right next to the station?</i>

202
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:29,360
<i>It was in those 200 meters</i>
<i>that he disappeared.</i>

203
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:33,240
<i>ETA was furious.</i>

204
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:37,040
<i>Ten days earlier, the Civil Guard</i>
<i>had freed a hostage held by ETA,</i>

205
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:38,680
<i>José Antonio Ortega Lara,</i>

206
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:43,120
<i>a prison officer who had been held hostage</i>
<i>for more than a year and a half.</i>

207
00:12:43,200 --> 00:12:46,520
<i>That was ETA's trump card</i>
<i>for blackmailing the government,</i>

208
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:48,600
<i>and it had lost it.</i>

209
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:52,680
<i>The terrorist response was coming.</i>
<i>We all knew that.</i>

210
00:12:52,760 --> 00:12:56,240
<i>What we did not know was how,</i>
<i>or against whom.</i>

211
00:12:56,760 --> 00:13:00,400
<i>Ortega Lara was freed</i>
<i>by the Civil Guard early this morning</i>

212
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:04,480
<i>from a hiding place in an industrial area</i>
<i>in Mondragón, Gipuzkoa.</i>

213
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:07,000
{\an8}JOSÉ ANTONIO ORTEGA LARA
RELEASED JULY 1, 1997

214
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:10,480
{\an8}We were still celebrating.

215
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:12,720
At that moment, we did not think

216
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:15,960
ETA would respond so quickly.

217
00:13:16,040 --> 00:13:18,200
And on July 10th,
happiness gave way to tragedy,

218
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:19,560
as it so often does in life.

219
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:22,080
But every victory has its price.

220
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:26,680
{\an8}And we paid it that afternoon,
July 10th, 1997.

221
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:27,880
{\an8}JULY 10, 1997

222
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:30,000
There will always be ups and downs,

223
00:13:30,080 --> 00:13:35,200
but we must remain steadfast
and persevere in our efforts.

224
00:13:35,280 --> 00:13:39,280
And that will be the government's policy.
Strength and determination.

225
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:43,240
And ETA could not let that go unanswered.

226
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:45,560
When would it happen?

227
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:57,560
More than 500 days to free Ortega Lara,

228
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:01,960
and 48 hours to find Miguel Ángel Blanco.

229
00:14:02,040 --> 00:14:03,120
It didn't look good.

230
00:14:03,200 --> 00:14:05,360
It certainly did not look good at all.

231
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:10,280
It was a task I would hesitate
to call impossible,

232
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:14,280
because Civil Guards
believe nothing is impossible.

233
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:19,120
He always said that
if ETA ever tried to kidnap him,

234
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:22,840
he would do everything he could
to either escape…

235
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:25,880
Because he would rather
be shot on the spot

236
00:14:25,960 --> 00:14:29,040
than be held captive
the way Ortega Lara was,

237
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:31,600
because he thought
he would not be able to bear it.

238
00:14:31,680 --> 00:14:32,800
THURSDAY
7:00 P.M.

239
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:34,640
46 HOURS LEFT

240
00:14:34,720 --> 00:14:38,880
Just as we were still
celebrating Ortega Lara's release,

241
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:40,760
the happy ending to his kidnapping,

242
00:14:40,840 --> 00:14:44,880
we were overwhelmed once again
by despair, unease, and uncertainty.

243
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:46,680
We are facing another ETA kidnapping.

244
00:14:46,760 --> 00:14:49,840
The motive was the same one
behind Ortega Lara's kidnapping.

245
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:52,720
To transfer Basque prisoners
to prisons in Euskadi.

246
00:14:53,640 --> 00:14:55,640
Back then, we used newswire machines

247
00:14:55,720 --> 00:14:58,080
that would beep for major stories,

248
00:14:58,160 --> 00:14:59,800
{\an8}usually grim ones.

249
00:14:59,880 --> 00:15:00,920
{\an8}JOURNALIST

250
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,200
{\an8}And I remember
the newswire machine beeping.

251
00:15:04,720 --> 00:15:08,280
That alert must have said,
"PP councilman kidnapped in Ermua."

252
00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:11,800
Right then, I knew

253
00:15:11,880 --> 00:15:16,080
this was the response
we'd been expecting from ETA

254
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:21,320
to José Antonio Ortega Lara's release.

255
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:24,160
And I knew something else.

256
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:28,800
Miguel Ángel's fate was sealed.

257
00:15:29,880 --> 00:15:32,040
I owned a café in Ermua.

258
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:35,680
{\an8}We were there and,
suddenly, I looked at the TV…

259
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:37,000
{\an8}MIGUEL'S FRIEND

260
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:41,120
…and saw an image, a map of the peninsula,

261
00:15:41,200 --> 00:15:43,920
{\an8}and Miguel's picture came up.

262
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,120
And I went into shock.
I couldn't remember anything.

263
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:51,880
I went into the storeroom.
They told me afterward what had happened.

264
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:54,800
I started crying. I had a panic attack.

265
00:15:55,320 --> 00:15:56,960
I mean, I couldn't believe it.

266
00:15:57,040 --> 00:15:59,520
A man down there said to me,

267
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:02,520
{\an8}"Don't you have a nephew
called Miguel Ángel Blanco?"

268
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:03,640
{\an8}TESTIMONY OF MIGUEL'S AUNT

269
00:16:03,720 --> 00:16:05,040
{\an8}I said, "Yes, we're the Blancos."

270
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:08,760
{\an8}And he said,
"Well, they've kidnapped him."

271
00:16:08,840 --> 00:16:10,640
And I said, "What?"

272
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:13,360
He said,
"Yes. They're saying it on the radio."

273
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:15,520
How did the family take the news?

274
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:18,160
I don't know.
I only just found out. I don't know.

275
00:16:18,240 --> 00:16:19,080
Thank you.

276
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:29,320
<i>-In Ermua, as…</i>
<i>-Miguel Blanco is…</i>

277
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:33,800
<i>That sleepy Spain,</i>
<i>on that hot July afternoon,</i>

278
00:16:33,880 --> 00:16:35,760
<i>had not reacted yet.</i>

279
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:38,840
<i>Many were already at the beach.</i>

280
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:43,200
<i>The rest of us, still working,</i>
<i>were dreaming of being there.</i>

281
00:16:43,840 --> 00:16:48,480
<i>The few people who had the radio on</i>
<i>spread the news by word of mouth,</i>

282
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:51,920
<i>the way people did before social media.</i>

283
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:55,600
<i>Some found out when they got home.</i>

284
00:16:55,680 --> 00:16:58,360
<i>Around that time,</i>
<i>a man was still up on some scaffolding,</i>

285
00:16:58,440 --> 00:17:01,280
<i>a working man who had not heard the news.</i>

286
00:17:01,880 --> 00:17:04,240
<i>His name was Miguel, like his son.</i>

287
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:10,720
As I was asking my colleagues,
a car pulled up and a man got out.

288
00:17:11,600 --> 00:17:12,960
He was coming home from work.

289
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:16,800
A construction worker.
Small, with white hair…

290
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:18,400
He walked toward the door,

291
00:17:18,480 --> 00:17:21,120
and there were two
Ertzaintza officers at the entrance.

292
00:17:21,200 --> 00:17:22,040
What's going on?

293
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:25,080
As he was about to go in,
a colleague asked him the question

294
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:27,560
none of us wanted to ask.

295
00:17:30,080 --> 00:17:32,560
-Are you a relative of Miguel Ángel's?
-Yes.

296
00:17:32,640 --> 00:17:33,520
Who are you?

297
00:17:34,040 --> 00:17:35,720
What happened?

298
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:37,680
-It seems he's been kidnapped.
-What?

299
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:40,680
It seems ETA has kidnapped him.

300
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:43,560
You didn't know anything?

301
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:46,360
I told the cameraman to keep filming,
but I didn't take out my mic.

302
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:47,720
I remember it perfectly.

303
00:17:48,320 --> 00:17:49,480
Are you his father?

304
00:17:50,640 --> 00:17:56,120
The Ertzaintza officers
immediately took him inside the building.

305
00:17:57,560 --> 00:17:58,680
That man…

306
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:03,200
His face said it all.

307
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:08,920
We did not know exactly what had happened
or who had been kidnapped.

308
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,440
That unknown councilman
we knew nothing about.

309
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:18,360
Let's take a moment to remember
the man we're all worried about.

310
00:18:18,440 --> 00:18:19,880
Miguel Ángel Blanco Garrido.

311
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:22,880
He was born in 1968, in Ermua, Biscay.

312
00:18:23,480 --> 00:18:27,440
He is 29 years old. He has been a member
of the Partido Popular since 1995.

313
00:18:27,520 --> 00:18:30,440
He ran in the last local elections.

314
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:32,800
His passion had always been the drums.

315
00:18:32,880 --> 00:18:36,560
I remember him
listening to music as a child

316
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:39,440
and pretending to play the drums.

317
00:18:39,520 --> 00:18:41,920
He even made his own drum kit

318
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,080
out of my mom's pots and pans.

319
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:48,840
I met them through music,

320
00:18:48,920 --> 00:18:54,160
because I was a DJ at a nightclub
that used to hire them on New Year's Eve.

321
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:55,440
I saw them play there.

322
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:57,600
Over time, they lost their bass player.

323
00:18:57,680 --> 00:19:00,800
By then,
I had stopped working at the nightclub,

324
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:03,520
precisely because my boss
had been murdered by ETA.

325
00:19:03,600 --> 00:19:06,120
He was a rather nervous guy.

326
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:11,120
And his eyes would twitch
when he got nervous.

327
00:19:11,640 --> 00:19:13,800
He hated it when someone made a mistake.

328
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:17,440
And during rehearsals,
he might suddenly snap.

329
00:19:17,520 --> 00:19:21,800
At gigs I saw drumsticks
go flying at more than one person,

330
00:19:21,880 --> 00:19:23,760
hitting them in the back.

331
00:19:23,840 --> 00:19:28,920
He was a guy who played drums in a band

332
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,520
because it helped him
make some extra cash.

333
00:19:33,920 --> 00:19:37,360
Miguel Ángel started in early 1997.

334
00:19:37,440 --> 00:19:40,640
At first, he was sort of an intern,

335
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:44,680
and then he helped out
in the accounting department.

336
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:49,560
And, well, he worked with us
for six or seven months.

337
00:19:49,640 --> 00:19:51,160
He was a very warm person.

338
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:56,520
I had been friends with his girlfriend,
Marimar, since we were little.

339
00:19:56,600 --> 00:20:00,040
The weekend right before it happened,

340
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:04,200
Miguel and Marimar came over on Sunday,
and I spent time with them.

341
00:20:04,280 --> 00:20:09,800
They were so excited.
They told me they'd bought a car.

342
00:20:09,880 --> 00:20:13,880
And she was always at rehearsals.

343
00:20:13,960 --> 00:20:16,680
Not just at gigs,
though she was there too.

344
00:20:16,760 --> 00:20:18,880
Every rehearsal.

345
00:20:18,960 --> 00:20:21,960
They were the kind of couple
who went everywhere together.

346
00:20:22,040 --> 00:20:24,560
I have always been
very interested in politics,

347
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:26,840
and so was my brother.

348
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:31,240
I remember when he told me
he was going to join the Partido Popular,

349
00:20:31,320 --> 00:20:33,000
I was really happy.

350
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:36,000
Yes, he liked it.
He liked doing things for his town.

351
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:40,440
But I never saw him
as that involved in politics.

352
00:20:40,520 --> 00:20:43,040
He cared more about his band,

353
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:47,440
going out, and his friends
than about politics.

354
00:20:47,520 --> 00:20:51,360
I never heard him say,
"When I'm mayor of Ermua…" Never.

355
00:20:51,440 --> 00:20:55,560
As a councilman, you got a small salary,
a certain amount each month

356
00:20:55,640 --> 00:20:57,800
to supplement your other job,
if you had one…

357
00:20:57,880 --> 00:21:01,880
{\an8}Whenever we talked politics,
it was usually to joke about something.

358
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:03,400
{\an8}We are going to respond to this.

359
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:04,680
{\an8}TOWN HALL MEETING ROOM

360
00:21:04,760 --> 00:21:07,040
{\an8}There is a general plan
to address all of this,

361
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:08,520
{\an8}but we also want to say

362
00:21:08,600 --> 00:21:11,560
{\an8}that we are
gathering residents' complaints,

363
00:21:11,640 --> 00:21:15,960
so we also ask that this issue
be addressed without further delay.

364
00:21:16,040 --> 00:21:19,880
It is clearly an issue
for the residents of Okin-Zuri,

365
00:21:19,960 --> 00:21:22,400
and we want to make that clear here.

366
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:28,760
{\an8}ASSEMBLY HOUSE

367
00:21:28,840 --> 00:21:32,480
{\an8}The first time I remember him
was when we were in Gernika.

368
00:21:32,560 --> 00:21:35,280
{\an8}We took a picture,
and they introduced me to him.

369
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:36,920
{\an8}After that, we got to know each other

370
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,280
{\an8}because our towns had issues in common

371
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:41,960
and because he worked in Eibar,
where I was a councilman.

372
00:21:42,040 --> 00:21:44,640
{\an8}He was interested
in doing things for the town.

373
00:21:44,720 --> 00:21:47,240
{\an8}He talked a lot about the sports center.

374
00:21:47,320 --> 00:21:48,520
{\an8}And he always said

375
00:21:48,600 --> 00:21:52,280
{\an8}they wanted to see if they could get
a proper sports center for the town.

376
00:21:52,360 --> 00:21:55,720
<i>…the collapse of</i>
<i>the municipal sports center on April 23rd…</i>

377
00:21:55,800 --> 00:21:58,840
And he fought very hard
for that sports center in Ermua.

378
00:21:59,440 --> 00:22:01,560
I was eight years older than Miguel Ángel.

379
00:22:01,640 --> 00:22:04,640
It was during the 1995 elections.

380
00:22:04,720 --> 00:22:05,920
Miguel Ángel and I

381
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:09,880
were handing out campaign leaflets
in San Pelayo Park, in Ermua.

382
00:22:09,960 --> 00:22:14,400
He had a biker-style jacket
that said "Total" on the back.

383
00:22:14,480 --> 00:22:20,120
A friend of mine and my brother
affectionately called him "Total"

384
00:22:20,200 --> 00:22:21,760
because he loved wearing that jacket.

385
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:24,840
We learned all of that
during those 48 hours,

386
00:22:24,920 --> 00:22:28,040
and as a journalist,
you put together a profile of him

387
00:22:28,120 --> 00:22:30,720
and grow fond of him. It's inevitable.

388
00:22:33,200 --> 00:22:36,600
He was a very young councilman with drive

389
00:22:36,680 --> 00:22:38,400
{\an8}and a clear willingness to take a stand…

390
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:39,840
{\an8}FORMER MAYOR OF ERMUA, PSOE

391
00:22:39,920 --> 00:22:42,000
{\an8}…because being a councilman
in the Basque Country

392
00:22:42,080 --> 00:22:44,440
{\an8}was already a difficult personal choice.

393
00:22:45,200 --> 00:22:46,400
But he had no experience.

394
00:22:46,480 --> 00:22:50,480
I remember that in January 1995,
when Gregorio Ordóñez was murdered,

395
00:22:50,560 --> 00:22:53,920
at rehearsal that week,
the band's singer came in last.

396
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:57,440
We were all already in our places,

397
00:22:57,520 --> 00:23:02,760
and he said to him, word for word,
"Watch yourself. Stay alert."

398
00:23:02,840 --> 00:23:05,160
And he laughed, because he said,

399
00:23:05,240 --> 00:23:09,120
"Come on. How important am I in politics?"

400
00:23:12,680 --> 00:23:13,800
<i>What others could see,</i>

401
00:23:13,880 --> 00:23:17,240
<i>his mother, his friends, his bandmates,</i>

402
00:23:17,320 --> 00:23:21,720
<i>Miguel did not see.</i>
<i>He was unaware of the danger.</i>

403
00:23:21,800 --> 00:23:24,960
<i>He had a fixed schedule, an office job,</i>

404
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:27,840
<i>and at night,</i>
<i>he rehearsed at the same place.</i>

405
00:23:28,360 --> 00:23:33,920
<i>An orderly, predictable life,</i>
<i>perfect as a terrorist target.</i>

406
00:23:34,520 --> 00:23:38,760
<i>We grew fond of someone</i>
<i>who could have been any one of us.</i>

407
00:23:40,280 --> 00:23:43,480
<i>ETA has given Miguel Ángel</i>
<i>48 hours to live.</i>

408
00:23:43,560 --> 00:23:44,400
THURSDAY
11:00 P.M.

409
00:23:44,480 --> 00:23:45,360
41 HOURS LEFT

410
00:23:45,440 --> 00:23:47,520
<i>Seven hours have already passed.</i>

411
00:23:48,320 --> 00:23:53,680
The first decision
was to circulate the hostage's photo

412
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:58,160
so that everyone, everywhere,
would see his face.

413
00:23:58,680 --> 00:24:00,360
We got a photo.

414
00:24:00,440 --> 00:24:02,520
We went to his mother's
and asked her for one.

415
00:24:02,600 --> 00:24:04,280
We managed to scan it.

416
00:24:04,360 --> 00:24:06,920
Back then, that was hard.
No one had a scanner.

417
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:09,120
We did it through someone I knew.

418
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:11,000
We made a little poster,

419
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:14,680
ran off copies on our photocopier,

420
00:24:14,760 --> 00:24:18,320
and stayed out until after midnight

421
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:21,160
putting them up around Eibar and Ermua.

422
00:24:21,240 --> 00:24:24,760
It was a way of keeping hope alive.
"Miguel, we are waiting for you."

423
00:24:24,840 --> 00:24:26,200
{\an8}MIGUEL, WE ARE WAITING FOR YOU

424
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:29,080
{\an8}I really broke down
when I arrived in Ermua

425
00:24:29,160 --> 00:24:31,600
and started seeing the whole town covered

426
00:24:31,680 --> 00:24:35,320
with my brother's photo and the phrase
"Miguel, we are waiting for you,"

427
00:24:35,400 --> 00:24:37,240
{\an8}in Spanish and Euskera.

428
00:24:37,880 --> 00:24:41,240
I realized that,
unfortunately, this was real.

429
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:46,320
{\an8}Those of us involved were well aware

430
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:48,240
that ETA was a threat to his life

431
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:51,840
and also a huge threat to us,

432
00:24:52,600 --> 00:24:55,080
to our lives and our freedom.

433
00:24:55,160 --> 00:24:58,080
We made the choice
to confront them head-on

434
00:24:58,160 --> 00:25:00,080
because some of us were sick and tired

435
00:25:00,160 --> 00:25:03,800
of the silence, the resignation,
and being humiliated by ETA.

436
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:11,480
MIGUEL, WE ARE WAITING FOR YOU

437
00:25:20,920 --> 00:25:24,080
<i>We have learned</i>
<i>that Miguel Ángel Blanco's sister,</i>

438
00:25:24,160 --> 00:25:25,800
<i>who was in England…</i>

439
00:25:25,880 --> 00:25:28,360
My house had a very long hallway.

440
00:25:28,440 --> 00:25:32,040
I walked into the kitchen,
where my parents were,

441
00:25:32,120 --> 00:25:35,000
and I remember hugging them…

442
00:25:36,960 --> 00:25:38,120
and saying, "I'm here now."

443
00:25:38,200 --> 00:25:40,320
FRIDAY
10:00 A.M.

444
00:25:40,400 --> 00:25:41,600
30 HOURS LEFT

445
00:25:41,680 --> 00:25:44,840
<i>Good morning.</i>
<i>Miguel Ángel Blanco Garrido, 29,</i>

446
00:25:44,920 --> 00:25:47,320
<i>a Partido Popular councilman in Ermua,</i>

447
00:25:47,400 --> 00:25:49,760
<i>has been held hostage by ETA for 18 hours.</i>

448
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:51,960
In response
to Miguel Ángel Blanco's kidnapping

449
00:25:52,040 --> 00:25:55,600
and the threats the terrorist group ETA
has made against his life,

450
00:25:55,680 --> 00:26:01,080
all television networks condemn
this latest attack on everyone's freedom.

451
00:26:01,160 --> 00:26:03,120
Across Spain,
we are displaying a single symbol.

452
00:26:03,200 --> 00:26:06,000
{\an8}The TVE logo is no longer
at the bottom right of your screen.

453
00:26:06,080 --> 00:26:09,080
{\an8}This blue ribbon,
which we thought we had left behind…

454
00:26:09,160 --> 00:26:11,240
A blue ribbon that best symbolizes

455
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:15,200
our shared defense of freedom
and rejection of terrorism.

456
00:26:15,280 --> 00:26:19,240
A blue ribbon of solidarity across Spain.
Thank you and good afternoon.

457
00:26:19,320 --> 00:26:23,520
MIGUEL, WE ARE WAITING FOR YOU

458
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:27,960
I personally took it upon myself

459
00:26:28,040 --> 00:26:31,080
to call Miguel Ángel Blanco's family,

460
00:26:31,160 --> 00:26:33,800
and that is what I did.

461
00:26:34,320 --> 00:26:36,800
I spoke with his sister,
I spoke with his parents,

462
00:26:37,440 --> 00:26:40,960
and I told them
the government will not give in.

463
00:26:41,480 --> 00:26:45,240
"The government
does not and cannot accept blackmail."

464
00:26:45,760 --> 00:26:51,160
"But we will do everything possible
to save Miguel Ángel Blanco."

465
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:53,000
The prime minister said to me,

466
00:26:53,080 --> 00:26:57,800
"You have to go on television
and explain exactly what is happening."

467
00:26:57,880 --> 00:27:00,080
And, of course, that is what I did.

468
00:27:00,160 --> 00:27:04,120
On the drive from Moncloa,
I prepared some notes

469
00:27:04,200 --> 00:27:08,160
for my statement
at that very difficult moment.

470
00:27:09,120 --> 00:27:10,640
I had to do it in half an hour.

471
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:14,480
I was in the car,
and I had to choose my words carefully.

472
00:27:14,560 --> 00:27:18,240
Simply put, we are facing coercion,

473
00:27:19,320 --> 00:27:20,920
a threat,

474
00:27:21,760 --> 00:27:22,800
blackmail.

475
00:27:22,880 --> 00:27:25,600
I have the speech written down.

476
00:27:25,680 --> 00:27:28,160
We were going to fulfill our duty.

477
00:27:29,320 --> 00:27:32,680
And I also meant to say
that it was our obligation.

478
00:27:33,280 --> 00:27:34,200
Thank you.

479
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:39,480
That statement was probably
the most important one I ever made

480
00:27:39,560 --> 00:27:44,920
because it was not
going to save Miguel Ángel Blanco's life,

481
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:48,960
but I could not make a mistake

482
00:27:49,040 --> 00:27:53,560
that might make
his murder easier to justify.

483
00:27:54,200 --> 00:27:58,600
It was one of the most difficult decisions
I made as prime minister,

484
00:27:59,120 --> 00:28:02,200
but I made it
fully aware of my responsibility.

485
00:28:02,280 --> 00:28:05,040
I am sure that, from the very beginning,

486
00:28:05,120 --> 00:28:09,280
they told him what his fate would be
once the 48 hours were up.

487
00:28:09,360 --> 00:28:12,480
I am sure they explained to him
from the very first minute

488
00:28:12,560 --> 00:28:16,040
what they were going to do to him.
I have no doubt at all.

489
00:28:16,560 --> 00:28:18,920
Absolutely sure, I mean…

490
00:28:19,680 --> 00:28:21,560
He was in the hands of real beasts.

491
00:28:21,640 --> 00:28:23,440
FRIDAY
2:00 P.M.

492
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:25,360
27 HOURS LEFT

493
00:28:25,440 --> 00:28:28,400
<i>Miguel Ángel Blanco has been</i>
<i>in ETA's hands for 22 hours.</i>

494
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:32,520
<i>Yesterday, the terrorists took control</i>
<i>of this young economist's fate…</i>

495
00:28:32,600 --> 00:28:35,600
<i>Thousands of people are taking</i>
<i>to the streets across the country</i>

496
00:28:35,680 --> 00:28:37,560
{\an8}<i>to try to stop this madness.</i>

497
00:28:37,640 --> 00:28:40,600
{\an8}What happened yesterday
was a terrible blow.

498
00:28:40,680 --> 00:28:46,000
{\an8}Let's hope this ends well
and doesn't end in tragedy.

499
00:28:47,440 --> 00:28:51,040
{\an8}A completely unforgivable crime.

500
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:55,400
{\an8}It's terrible to take away
someone's freedom,

501
00:28:55,480 --> 00:28:57,080
especially with threats like that.

502
00:28:59,760 --> 00:29:01,880
{\an8}Unthinkable that this could happen again.

503
00:29:01,960 --> 00:29:04,000
{\an8}<i>We will win, ETA will lose!</i>

504
00:29:04,080 --> 00:29:06,040
{\an8}Please, don't let them kill him.

505
00:29:06,120 --> 00:29:09,480
{\an8}Let them talk and stop this nonsense.
This is not a war.

506
00:29:11,080 --> 00:29:12,000
{\an8}<i>Meanwhile,</i>

507
00:29:12,080 --> 00:29:14,960
{\an8}<i>as the protests swell</i>
<i>into a chorus of voices,</i>

508
00:29:15,040 --> 00:29:17,560
{\an8}<i>the security forces</i>
<i>are working against the clock</i>

509
00:29:17,640 --> 00:29:19,680
<i>to find the hostage.</i>

510
00:29:20,200 --> 00:29:24,960
{\an8}We began reaching out
to all our sources and contacts

511
00:29:25,040 --> 00:29:28,120
{\an8}for any information we could get.

512
00:29:29,320 --> 00:29:32,480
The public mobilization
was extremely important.

513
00:29:32,560 --> 00:29:34,440
People had to get involved,

514
00:29:34,520 --> 00:29:39,120
because ETA and HB had created

515
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:42,640
a climate of silence
and fear in Euskadi at the time.

516
00:29:42,720 --> 00:29:48,160
As expected, all political groups
except Herri Batasuna have unanimously

517
00:29:48,240 --> 00:29:51,040
condemned and rejected the violence.

518
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:55,920
It is incredible, but there are still
basic ideals worth fighting for,

519
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,200
worth suffering for, even dying for.

520
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:04,080
We came to the firm conclusion
that ETA would not get away with it.

521
00:30:04,160 --> 00:30:05,440
We were going to respond,

522
00:30:05,520 --> 00:30:08,840
and, of course, silence and resignation

523
00:30:08,920 --> 00:30:10,960
were not an option in Ermua.

524
00:30:11,040 --> 00:30:15,240
Someone might notice that a person has
stopped going to work regularly,

525
00:30:15,320 --> 00:30:18,960
changed their routine,
made an unusual comment,

526
00:30:19,040 --> 00:30:21,520
requested a vehicle
when they already have one,

527
00:30:21,600 --> 00:30:24,160
or asked to use a home
or ground-floor unit

528
00:30:24,240 --> 00:30:27,040
for no apparent reason.

529
00:30:27,120 --> 00:30:30,800
"Let's take to the hills.
If there is any movement, we'll see it."

530
00:30:30,880 --> 00:30:33,120
People did not want to stay at home.

531
00:30:33,200 --> 00:30:37,600
People wanted to do something
to try to save my brother's life.

532
00:30:40,520 --> 00:30:42,600
The atmosphere in that house…

533
00:30:42,680 --> 00:30:44,760
{\an8}Chelo cooked, Chelo asked us…

534
00:30:44,840 --> 00:30:46,080
{\an8}ERTZAINTZA OFFICER

535
00:30:46,160 --> 00:30:49,360
{\an8}Miguel, the father,
was completely devastated.

536
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:52,600
I think he had a clearer sense
of what was happening.

537
00:30:52,680 --> 00:30:54,600
Of course, during those 48 hours,

538
00:30:54,680 --> 00:31:01,000
our family of four became a huge family.

539
00:31:01,080 --> 00:31:03,600
{\an8}And then I remember arriving
at the Blanco family's house…

540
00:31:03,680 --> 00:31:04,800
{\an8}FORMER ERMUA COUNCILMAN, PP

541
00:31:04,880 --> 00:31:05,720
{\an8}…and staying there,

542
00:31:05,800 --> 00:31:11,520
telling the white lies
you have to tell in situations like that.

543
00:31:14,160 --> 00:31:18,440
We felt the urgency
of trying to find him before 4:00 p.m.

544
00:31:18,520 --> 00:31:19,920
Precisely because

545
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:23,320
everyone knew there were
no other possibilities.

546
00:31:23,400 --> 00:31:25,680
FRIDAY
4:00 P.M.

547
00:31:25,760 --> 00:31:27,720
24 HOURS LEFT

548
00:31:28,480 --> 00:31:30,160
<i>It is 4:00 in the afternoon.</i>

549
00:31:30,240 --> 00:31:33,520
<i>There are 24 hours left</i>
<i>before ETA's deadline expires.</i>

550
00:31:34,320 --> 00:31:37,560
Many colleagues came to do live reports

551
00:31:37,640 --> 00:31:39,480
{\an8}and prepare pieces for the news.

552
00:31:39,560 --> 00:31:42,080
{\an8}And somehow, you and I were taken off

553
00:31:42,160 --> 00:31:44,760
{\an8}the regular news rundown

554
00:31:44,840 --> 00:31:48,800
because we were the ones with the sources,

555
00:31:48,880 --> 00:31:53,520
trying to work out
what might happen in the next few hours.

556
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:55,960
Where they might release him,
if they released him,

557
00:31:56,040 --> 00:31:57,440
or where they might kill him.

558
00:31:57,520 --> 00:32:00,040
Look, you and I
were there to cover the outcome.

559
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:02,960
I mean, this fell to us.

560
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:07,720
{\an8}So, with a map spread out over the office,

561
00:32:07,800 --> 00:32:11,920
{\an8}if you remember,
we analyzed the most recent kidnappings.

562
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:16,440
{\an8}There had been four kidnappings
around Eibar in the previous two years,

563
00:32:16,520 --> 00:32:21,360
{\an8}and the releases… All very close by.
It was only about 12 miles…

564
00:32:21,440 --> 00:32:23,200
Eibar was a hot spot.

565
00:32:23,280 --> 00:32:25,640
The area around Eibar,
and the surrounding mountains.

566
00:32:26,640 --> 00:32:29,040
<i>Against the clock,</i>
<i>the various police forces</i>

567
00:32:29,120 --> 00:32:31,080
<i>searched the entire region.</i>

568
00:32:31,160 --> 00:32:34,280
<i>The many checkpoints yielded no results.</i>

569
00:32:35,520 --> 00:32:37,680
I had been in charge for about a year

570
00:32:37,760 --> 00:32:40,080
of the public safety patrols in Donosti,

571
00:32:40,160 --> 00:32:42,880
{\an8}receiving all kinds of tips
from the public.

572
00:32:42,960 --> 00:32:44,400
{\an8}ERTZAINTZA SUPERINTENDENT

573
00:32:44,480 --> 00:32:47,800
{\an8}From vehicles people
thought they had seen that day…

574
00:32:47,880 --> 00:32:49,200
Someone who had heard on TV

575
00:32:49,280 --> 00:32:52,320
that Miguel Ángel Blanco
had been kidnapped.

576
00:32:52,400 --> 00:32:55,320
Someone had seen a strange vehicle

577
00:32:55,400 --> 00:33:00,400
with a person inside
making a suspicious gesture.

578
00:33:00,480 --> 00:33:01,760
Someone else said,

579
00:33:01,840 --> 00:33:04,600
"I saw a light at an odd hour
in some farmhouse."

580
00:33:04,680 --> 00:33:09,080
It was almost an exercise in brute force,
trying everything

581
00:33:09,160 --> 00:33:13,920
until we knocked on the right door
and someone opened it.

582
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:16,600
But it was like looking
for a needle in a haystack.

583
00:33:16,680 --> 00:33:18,160
Everyone worked nonstop.

584
00:33:18,240 --> 00:33:22,280
Vacations were canceled,
leave was canceled…

585
00:33:22,360 --> 00:33:23,880
Everyone was available.

586
00:33:23,960 --> 00:33:27,560
{\an8}We searched anything
that could be used to transport someone…

587
00:33:27,640 --> 00:33:29,160
{\an8}FORMER HEAD OF ERTZAINTZA OPERATIONS

588
00:33:29,240 --> 00:33:30,640
{\an8}…whether in a trunk

589
00:33:30,720 --> 00:33:33,520
inside a refrigerator, in a container…

590
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:36,240
Any kind of vehicle or compartment.

591
00:33:36,320 --> 00:33:40,840
{\an8}I think circulating the hostage's photo
was a good decision,

592
00:33:40,920 --> 00:33:44,520
because it was
broadcast immediately on television

593
00:33:44,600 --> 00:33:46,880
and also appeared in the newspapers.

594
00:33:46,960 --> 00:33:52,480
And that got many people to call in,

595
00:33:52,560 --> 00:33:55,720
which meant we had a basis

596
00:33:55,800 --> 00:33:59,560
for organizing raids and searches
in countless places.

597
00:33:59,640 --> 00:34:01,240
With hundreds of raids.

598
00:34:01,320 --> 00:34:04,080
Hundreds and hundreds of searches.

599
00:34:04,160 --> 00:34:08,680
Towns and entire areas
were combed through,

600
00:34:08,760 --> 00:34:11,240
almost farmhouse by farmhouse,
house by house…

601
00:34:11,320 --> 00:34:14,160
But we wanted to be sure
we had checked this house,

602
00:34:14,240 --> 00:34:18,080
this ground-floor unit, this <i>txoko</i>…

603
00:34:18,160 --> 00:34:20,800
What we tried to do,
and largely succeeded in doing,

604
00:34:20,880 --> 00:34:22,520
was monitor

605
00:34:22,600 --> 00:34:26,560
all the public phone booths
throughout Euskadi.

606
00:34:26,640 --> 00:34:30,000
If a call was made from a booth

607
00:34:30,080 --> 00:34:34,240
claiming responsibility
or mentioning the kidnapping

608
00:34:34,320 --> 00:34:37,440
or any subsequent action,
we could locate it immediately.

609
00:34:37,520 --> 00:34:40,440
Almost every phone booth
was being monitored.

610
00:34:45,040 --> 00:34:48,960
You don't want them to kill him,
because he has become part of you.

611
00:34:49,040 --> 00:34:52,040
Part of those of us reporting it,
trying not to cry,

612
00:34:52,120 --> 00:34:54,680
as I am trying not to cry now.

613
00:34:55,600 --> 00:34:59,000
You try not to let that happen,
but you grow fond of him.

614
00:34:59,080 --> 00:35:03,360
{\an8}CARDENAL ORBE SQUARE

615
00:35:09,320 --> 00:35:10,920
And it felt

616
00:35:11,720 --> 00:35:13,720
closer than usual, you know?

617
00:35:13,800 --> 00:35:18,320
He was someone our age.

618
00:35:19,760 --> 00:35:23,960
And as they told us
more and more about his life,

619
00:35:24,040 --> 00:35:29,760
those details became
part of how we saw him.

620
00:35:29,840 --> 00:35:32,200
We got to know him,

621
00:35:32,960 --> 00:35:34,520
to know his family,

622
00:35:35,240 --> 00:35:40,200
his interests, his life, basically.

623
00:35:41,080 --> 00:35:44,320
And that made it all
feel much closer to home.

624
00:35:44,400 --> 00:35:46,240
{\an8}STATEMENT BY MARIMAR BLANCO
CARDENAL ORBE SQUARE

625
00:35:46,320 --> 00:35:47,880
{\an8}Hello, Miguel Ángel.

626
00:35:48,600 --> 00:35:52,760
{\an8}Wherever you are,
we want you to know that we are with you,

627
00:35:53,280 --> 00:35:56,040
{\an8}that we all support you
and are waiting for you.

628
00:36:01,720 --> 00:36:03,240
I'd like to take this opportunity

629
00:36:03,320 --> 00:36:07,040
to call on the people of Ermua,
all of Euskadi,

630
00:36:07,880 --> 00:36:09,480
and the rest of Spain

631
00:36:09,560 --> 00:36:13,480
to come to their local squares
with candles

632
00:36:15,800 --> 00:36:20,240
{\an8}at midnight tonight, to bring light
to the darkness we are living through.

633
00:36:20,320 --> 00:36:21,600
Thank you very much.

634
00:36:32,360 --> 00:36:34,400
SATURDAY
1:00 A.M.

635
00:36:34,480 --> 00:36:37,160
15 HOURS LEFT

636
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:40,480
Breaking news.
Candles were lit at midnight,

637
00:36:40,560 --> 00:36:43,160
and are still being lit at this hour,

638
00:36:43,240 --> 00:36:46,960
in town and city squares across Spain.

639
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:50,960
Candlelight against the darkness
ETA wants to force upon us.

640
00:36:51,480 --> 00:36:57,120
Days of great sadness,
great anguish. Very sad days.

641
00:36:58,360 --> 00:37:01,200
{\an8}<i>Half of Spain goes to bed shaken.</i>

642
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:04,880
{\an8}<i>Many gather in silent vigils</i>
<i>across the country.</i>

643
00:37:05,400 --> 00:37:08,240
<i>But the Blanco family</i>
<i>spends a sleepless night.</i>

644
00:37:09,280 --> 00:37:12,360
<i>In Eibar,</i>
<i>barely two and a half miles away,</i>

645
00:37:12,440 --> 00:37:14,760
<i>a man named Ibon Muñoa is asleep,</i>

646
00:37:14,840 --> 00:37:19,280
<i>the informant who watched Miguel</i>
<i>and hid the killers.</i>

647
00:37:19,360 --> 00:37:21,000
<i>Because in Euskadi at the time,</i>

648
00:37:21,080 --> 00:37:24,200
<i>your neighbor,</i>
<i>the waitress at your favorite bar,</i>

649
00:37:24,280 --> 00:37:26,720
<i>an office coworker, or the local butcher,</i>

650
00:37:26,800 --> 00:37:30,000
<i>ordinary people</i>
<i>in your immediate surroundings,</i>

651
00:37:30,080 --> 00:37:32,720
<i>could be ETA informants.</i>

652
00:37:35,080 --> 00:37:38,760
I attended the Eibar town council meeting,
where I was a councilman,

653
00:37:38,840 --> 00:37:42,360
and we wanted the town hall
to condemn the kidnapping

654
00:37:42,440 --> 00:37:44,520
and call for Miguel Ángel's release.

655
00:37:45,320 --> 00:37:48,960
And at that moment,
I was sitting next to the HB councilman,

656
00:37:49,040 --> 00:37:50,280
Ibon Muñoa.

657
00:37:50,360 --> 00:37:55,600
He was a fairly quiet person,
but he seemed very withdrawn.

658
00:37:55,680 --> 00:37:57,680
I think he was very easy to manipulate,

659
00:37:57,760 --> 00:37:59,880
and perhaps that was
what ETA was looking for.

660
00:37:59,960 --> 00:38:01,720
That kind of collaborator.

661
00:38:01,800 --> 00:38:05,360
At that moment, I could not imagine
that the person next to me

662
00:38:05,440 --> 00:38:09,480
was the terrorist who had
passed information to the commando,

663
00:38:09,560 --> 00:38:12,920
sheltered its members in his own home,

664
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:16,600
and helped them
kill my colleague Miguel Ángel.

665
00:38:16,680 --> 00:38:18,800
It is blackmail. It is extortion.

666
00:38:19,320 --> 00:38:22,480
Murder means killing someone,
not "causing their death."

667
00:38:22,560 --> 00:38:26,880
I sometimes say that we Basques
have a problem building sentences.

668
00:38:26,960 --> 00:38:29,880
As journalists know, proper sentences

669
00:38:29,960 --> 00:38:32,560
have a subject, a verb, and an object.

670
00:38:32,640 --> 00:38:35,720
And sometimes we find it very hard
to state the subject and the verb.

671
00:38:35,800 --> 00:38:37,880
"ETA kills." Who?

672
00:38:38,680 --> 00:38:41,160
Sometimes we twisted the sentence around
and made it seem

673
00:38:41,240 --> 00:38:44,160
as if someone had simply turned up dead,

674
00:38:44,240 --> 00:38:46,280
as if struck by lightning. No.

675
00:38:46,360 --> 00:38:50,080
Someone carried it out,
someone tipped them off beforehand,

676
00:38:50,160 --> 00:38:52,240
someone sheltered them afterward.

677
00:38:53,240 --> 00:38:55,040
<i>We later found out</i>

678
00:38:55,120 --> 00:38:56,320
<i>that ETA's leader,</i>

679
00:38:56,400 --> 00:38:59,280
<i>José Javier Arizkuren Ruiz,</i>
<i>alias Kantauri,</i>

680
00:38:59,360 --> 00:39:02,840
<i>had given his operatives</i>
<i>the following written order.</i>

681
00:39:02,920 --> 00:39:05,800
<i>"Any PP politician is a target."</i>

682
00:39:05,880 --> 00:39:08,040
<i>"Put all your effort</i>
<i>into abducting a councilman</i>

683
00:39:08,120 --> 00:39:10,520
<i>and give them a few days</i>
<i>to move the prisoners</i>

684
00:39:10,600 --> 00:39:12,120
<i>to Euskadi."</i>

685
00:39:12,200 --> 00:39:13,480
<i>"Do it as soon as possible."</i>

686
00:39:13,560 --> 00:39:16,680
<i>"And if you cannot kidnap him</i>
<i>or something goes wrong,</i>

687
00:39:16,760 --> 00:39:19,680
<i>hit them as hard as you can."</i>

688
00:39:20,280 --> 00:39:23,040
<i>The text oozes hatred and resentment.</i>

689
00:39:23,560 --> 00:39:26,800
<i>ETA follows the playbook</i>
<i>I had already seen in other conflicts.</i>

690
00:39:26,880 --> 00:39:31,360
<i>First, you dehumanize your enemy,</i>
<i>and then you call for their annihilation.</i>

691
00:39:31,440 --> 00:39:33,160
SATURDAY
8:00 A.M.

692
00:39:33,240 --> 00:39:34,480
9 HOURS LEFT

693
00:39:34,560 --> 00:39:36,600
<i>Good morning. It has been 40 hours</i>

694
00:39:36,680 --> 00:39:39,680
<i>since Miguel Ángel Blanco</i>
<i>was kidnapped by ETA.</i>

695
00:39:39,760 --> 00:39:42,000
Freedom!

696
00:39:42,080 --> 00:39:48,560
Basques yes, ETA no!

697
00:39:48,640 --> 00:39:52,920
They wanted to humiliate us and impose
their totalitarian political agenda,

698
00:39:53,000 --> 00:39:56,040
but they knew full well
that we were going to fight back

699
00:39:56,120 --> 00:40:00,200
and that our response would not be
one of silence and resignation,

700
00:40:00,280 --> 00:40:01,840
but the exact opposite.

701
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:04,000
Citizens, politicians, the Church,

702
00:40:04,080 --> 00:40:06,320
unions, civic groups…

703
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:10,120
Everyone, without exception,
has spent this slow, agonizing day

704
00:40:10,200 --> 00:40:12,440
reminding ETA that its fight is pointless,

705
00:40:12,520 --> 00:40:14,840
that it is up against an entire country.

706
00:40:14,920 --> 00:40:18,400
The public response across Spain
was so overwhelming,

707
00:40:18,920 --> 00:40:23,080
so impressive, that many believed

708
00:40:23,160 --> 00:40:28,200
it would affect the terrorists' morale.

709
00:40:28,280 --> 00:40:29,400
BASQUES YES, ETA NO!

710
00:40:29,480 --> 00:40:32,600
Freedom!

711
00:40:32,680 --> 00:40:34,840
DON'T KILL HIM

712
00:40:35,760 --> 00:40:37,480
The message could be:

713
00:40:38,240 --> 00:40:42,880
{\an8}Miguel Ángel is not alone. ETA is alone.

714
00:40:42,960 --> 00:40:46,040
{\an8}STATEMENT BY IÑAKI GABILONDO
MADRID'S PUERTA DEL SOL

715
00:40:46,120 --> 00:40:48,000
FREEDOM

716
00:40:48,080 --> 00:40:51,120
{\an8}We want to tell the government
and the people holding my brother…

717
00:40:51,200 --> 00:40:52,400
{\an8}ERNESTO ERKOREKA SQUARE

718
00:40:52,480 --> 00:40:55,760
…that everything in life
can be solved with goodwill.

719
00:40:58,200 --> 00:41:02,040
By Saturday,
my mother was completely devastated.

720
00:41:02,120 --> 00:41:04,960
She was sitting on the sofa
in our living room.

721
00:41:05,040 --> 00:41:07,840
I remember arriving,
hugging her, and saying,

722
00:41:07,920 --> 00:41:10,240
"Mom, we've managed to save Miguel Ángel."

723
00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:13,400
PEACE NOW AND FOREVER

724
00:41:16,080 --> 00:41:18,920
The family is understandably anguished
as these hours pass.

725
00:41:19,000 --> 00:41:21,520
Meanwhile, the harrowing deadline
is ticking away…

726
00:41:21,600 --> 00:41:25,320
And I know everyone was horrified.

727
00:41:25,840 --> 00:41:29,800
{\an8}I think nothing could compare
to experiencing it there firsthand…

728
00:41:29,880 --> 00:41:31,160
{\an8}JOURNALIST

729
00:41:31,240 --> 00:41:35,440
{\an8}…to being there, breathing the same air

730
00:41:35,520 --> 00:41:38,680
as Miguel Ángel Blanco's family,

731
00:41:38,760 --> 00:41:42,760
breathing the same air
as his neighbors, his friends,

732
00:41:43,920 --> 00:41:47,760
and, sadly, the same air the terrorists
who kidnapped him had breathed.

733
00:41:47,840 --> 00:41:50,320
Words are not enough.

734
00:41:50,400 --> 00:41:54,080
{\an8}The shows of solidarity
with Miguel Ángel Blanco Garrido continue

735
00:41:54,160 --> 00:41:56,880
with only two hours left

736
00:41:56,960 --> 00:42:00,680
before the ultimatum
issued by the terrorist group ETA expires.

737
00:42:11,480 --> 00:42:15,560
As the hours went by,
we began to think about it differently.

738
00:42:15,640 --> 00:42:18,640
He was no longer being held by a cell
planning a long-term kidnapping,

739
00:42:18,720 --> 00:42:21,160
but by an execution commando.

740
00:42:21,760 --> 00:42:24,480
I think that was key.

741
00:42:24,560 --> 00:42:30,280
You and I kept going over it,
thinking along those lines…

742
00:42:30,360 --> 00:42:34,800
Because holding someone
for 48 hours and then killing him…

743
00:42:34,880 --> 00:42:39,760
In their eyes, it had to be
a high-ranking, full-time ETA operative.

744
00:42:39,840 --> 00:42:42,440
-This is not a job for a legal member.
-A killer.

745
00:42:42,520 --> 00:42:43,840
This takes a killer.

746
00:42:43,920 --> 00:42:46,840
Someone with rank,
who knew exactly what they were doing,

747
00:42:46,920 --> 00:42:49,080
because this was cold-blooded murder.

748
00:42:50,040 --> 00:42:53,320
We also analyzed the situation,

749
00:42:53,400 --> 00:42:57,920
looking at ETA's
latest attacks in the area.

750
00:42:58,000 --> 00:43:00,280
And that pointed us
to the Donosti commando.

751
00:43:00,360 --> 00:43:02,200
It had to be them.

752
00:43:03,040 --> 00:43:07,680
We knew the Donosti commando
had already been active in the province.

753
00:43:08,840 --> 00:43:13,240
There were already indications
that Txapote and Amaia

754
00:43:13,760 --> 00:43:15,720
might be members of that commando.

755
00:43:15,800 --> 00:43:19,120
They had already shown,
if I remember correctly,

756
00:43:19,200 --> 00:43:22,200
that they carried out
point-blank executions,

757
00:43:22,280 --> 00:43:27,800
attacks that required
that kind of savagery,

758
00:43:27,880 --> 00:43:32,280
getting close to your victim
and putting a gun to their head.

759
00:43:41,880 --> 00:43:44,640
<i>The official account over the years</i>

760
00:43:44,720 --> 00:43:47,840
<i>is that the government</i>
<i>decided not to give in to blackmail</i>

761
00:43:47,920 --> 00:43:50,560
<i>or negotiate</i>
<i>over Miguel Ángel Blanco's life.</i>

762
00:43:50,640 --> 00:43:53,320
<i>But did no one really do anything?</i>

763
00:43:54,160 --> 00:43:55,360
<i>Did no one try?</i>

764
00:43:56,720 --> 00:43:57,560
<i>Yes.</i>

765
00:43:58,080 --> 00:44:01,760
<i>Some people risked everything</i>
<i>during those 48 hours,</i>

766
00:44:01,840 --> 00:44:04,760
<i>approaching the group</i>
<i>at the risk of their lives</i>

767
00:44:04,840 --> 00:44:07,120
<i>and of being accused of being traitors</i>

768
00:44:07,200 --> 00:44:08,840
<i>or government agents.</i>

769
00:44:10,240 --> 00:44:12,200
We did not assign anyone to do anything,

770
00:44:12,280 --> 00:44:18,160
but we did contact everyone
who might be able to help.

771
00:44:18,240 --> 00:44:20,080
{\an8}It is very hard to believe…

772
00:44:20,160 --> 00:44:21,000
{\an8}BISHOP OF BILBAO

773
00:44:21,080 --> 00:44:23,400
{\an8}…first, that the message got through,

774
00:44:23,480 --> 00:44:25,400
and second, that ETA

775
00:44:25,480 --> 00:44:28,320
would reverse a decision
it had already made.

776
00:44:28,400 --> 00:44:33,520
I know because the following year,
in 1998, I had to act as an intermediary

777
00:44:33,600 --> 00:44:38,800
in the context of an ETA ceasefire,
at the Spanish government's request,

778
00:44:38,880 --> 00:44:42,000
and that was when
I discovered how these things work.

779
00:44:42,080 --> 00:44:43,440
{\an8}FORMER PNV MEMBER OF CONGRESS

780
00:44:43,520 --> 00:44:47,160
{\an8}Well, we had a certain back channel

781
00:44:47,240 --> 00:44:48,440
{\an8}to that world.

782
00:44:48,520 --> 00:44:52,320
I know that the president of the PNV,
Xabier Arzalluz,

783
00:44:52,400 --> 00:44:57,000
asked Gorka Agirre,
who handled that kind of thing,

784
00:44:57,080 --> 00:45:00,040
to sound them out and see
if there was any possibility.

785
00:45:00,120 --> 00:45:03,920
But in the end,
they did send us a message saying,

786
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:05,520
"They are going to kill him."

787
00:45:07,400 --> 00:45:10,480
Long live ETA's military wing!

788
00:45:10,560 --> 00:45:12,560
I was in Herri Batasuna.

789
00:45:13,240 --> 00:45:18,520
I was part of a pacifist wing
of the Basque nationalist left.

790
00:45:18,600 --> 00:45:22,760
<i>Congressman Patxi Zabaleta</i>
<i>urgently asks the terrorist group</i>

791
00:45:22,840 --> 00:45:24,880
{\an8}<i>to spare the hostage's life.</i>

792
00:45:24,960 --> 00:45:28,320
{\an8}I got in the car and went to a town…

793
00:45:28,400 --> 00:45:29,680
{\an8}FORMER HERRI BATASUNA LEADER

794
00:45:29,760 --> 00:45:32,480
{\an8}…called Baigorri, in Iparralde,

795
00:45:33,000 --> 00:45:38,200
an hour and a half from here,
across the French border.

796
00:45:38,280 --> 00:45:42,240
There was no way for someone
from the Basque nationalist left

797
00:45:42,320 --> 00:45:45,440
to get a message to ETA that quickly.

798
00:45:45,520 --> 00:45:47,920
Communication was difficult

799
00:45:48,000 --> 00:45:51,000
for security reasons and other reasons.

800
00:45:51,080 --> 00:45:57,680
And there I met with "Peixoto,"
José Manuel Pagoaga Gallastegi,

801
00:45:57,760 --> 00:45:59,560
who had once been

802
00:45:59,640 --> 00:46:03,160
a member of ETA's leadership,

803
00:46:03,240 --> 00:46:07,120
and I asked him to do whatever he could

804
00:46:07,200 --> 00:46:11,360
to change the decision,
or at least influence

805
00:46:11,440 --> 00:46:13,320
ETA's leaders.

806
00:46:13,400 --> 00:46:15,120
He made a call,

807
00:46:15,200 --> 00:46:17,720
but there was not enough time

808
00:46:17,800 --> 00:46:20,880
or the right conditions
to accomplish anything.

809
00:46:20,960 --> 00:46:24,440
They are not Basques, they are murderers!

810
00:46:24,520 --> 00:46:26,120
<i>As Zabaleta found out,</i>

811
00:46:26,200 --> 00:46:29,160
{\an8}<i>not everyone inside ETA</i>
<i>agreed with that kidnapping.</i>

812
00:46:29,240 --> 00:46:30,880
{\an8}FORMER NANCLARES DE LA OCA PRISON

813
00:46:30,960 --> 00:46:34,160
{\an8}<i>In some prisons,</i>
<i>there was even dissent, some of it public,</i>

814
00:46:34,240 --> 00:46:36,160
{\an8}<i>and a great deal of internal opposition.</i>

815
00:46:36,240 --> 00:46:38,800
<i>Miguel!</i>

816
00:46:38,880 --> 00:46:43,040
All the ETA prisoners at the facility

817
00:46:43,120 --> 00:46:48,640
came out against the kidnapping
and murder of Miguel Ángel Blanco.

818
00:46:48,720 --> 00:46:51,480
{\an8}Many of them out of moral conviction…

819
00:46:51,560 --> 00:46:52,920
{\an8}FORMER DIRECTOR OF THE PRISON

820
00:46:53,000 --> 00:46:57,000
{\an8}…and others, I suspect,
because they considered it a mistake.

821
00:46:57,080 --> 00:47:00,080
{\an8}When, on July 21, 1995,

822
00:47:00,160 --> 00:47:03,240
{\an8}I voluntarily turned myself in
to the Spanish authorities,

823
00:47:04,080 --> 00:47:08,120
I said in an open letter
that terrorism was not my path,

824
00:47:08,200 --> 00:47:09,920
that ETA had lost its way.

825
00:47:10,560 --> 00:47:13,560
They need to hear that, yes,
I may be the first madman,

826
00:47:14,080 --> 00:47:16,480
but the last person
to leave ETA will be a fool.

827
00:47:16,560 --> 00:47:17,400
{\an8}CIRCULAR SQUARE

828
00:47:17,480 --> 00:47:19,680
{\an8}<i>Time is running out.</i>

829
00:47:19,760 --> 00:47:22,480
<i>While all of Spain</i>
<i>is demonstrating in the streets,</i>

830
00:47:22,560 --> 00:47:25,400
{\an8}<i>that same morning, a lawyer from Donosti,</i>

831
00:47:25,480 --> 00:47:28,480
{\an8}<i>with a small handbag</i>
<i>and great determination,</i>

832
00:47:28,560 --> 00:47:30,960
{\an8}<i>took a flight from Biarritz to Paris.</i>

833
00:47:32,320 --> 00:47:34,280
<i>In a cell at La Santé Prison,</i>

834
00:47:34,360 --> 00:47:38,200
<i>the former ideological leader of ETA</i>
<i>spends his time praying.</i>

835
00:47:38,280 --> 00:47:40,760
<i>José Luis Álvarez Santacristina,</i>
<i>alias Txelis,</i>

836
00:47:41,600 --> 00:47:45,240
<i>whom she has known since she was young,</i>

837
00:47:45,840 --> 00:47:48,960
<i>may be the last chance to save Miguel.</i>

838
00:47:49,040 --> 00:47:50,760
SATURDAY
3:00 P.M.

839
00:47:50,840 --> 00:47:53,080
1 HOUR LEFT

840
00:47:56,600 --> 00:47:59,960
Someone called me.

841
00:48:00,040 --> 00:48:01,000
{\an8}LAWYER

842
00:48:01,080 --> 00:48:03,800
{\an8}A very well-known journalist.

843
00:48:03,880 --> 00:48:05,800
We had been friends for years.

844
00:48:05,880 --> 00:48:08,040
He asked whether…

845
00:48:08,560 --> 00:48:11,560
He knew I had a relationship with Txelis,

846
00:48:11,640 --> 00:48:14,240
one of the members of ETA.

847
00:48:14,320 --> 00:48:16,640
He asked if I could do anything.

848
00:48:16,720 --> 00:48:18,640
It is true that earlier…

849
00:48:19,760 --> 00:48:23,200
The editor of <i>El Mundo </i>at the time,
Pedro J. Ramírez,

850
00:48:23,840 --> 00:48:28,040
asked us whether
it would be possible to meet

851
00:48:28,600 --> 00:48:31,240
with a member of ETA's leadership.

852
00:48:32,560 --> 00:48:35,400
He asked us to make arrangements
with the French authorities

853
00:48:35,480 --> 00:48:37,960
so they would allow it.

854
00:48:38,480 --> 00:48:39,800
I agreed,

855
00:48:39,880 --> 00:48:43,080
but only as long
as he understood one thing.

856
00:48:43,600 --> 00:48:45,560
He would not be acting
on behalf of the government,

857
00:48:45,640 --> 00:48:48,400
and this would not be a mediation effort.

858
00:48:48,480 --> 00:48:52,480
They allowed it. They made
it very clear to me that this was…

859
00:48:53,440 --> 00:48:58,240
well, a private initiative.

860
00:48:58,880 --> 00:49:00,880
The government
wanted nothing to do with it.

861
00:49:00,960 --> 00:49:05,440
It was Chevènement
who gave me permission to enter.

862
00:49:05,520 --> 00:49:07,440
-The French interior minister.
-Yes.

863
00:49:07,520 --> 00:49:10,400
He authorized it. How else
would I have gotten into the prison?

864
00:49:10,480 --> 00:49:13,160
All I did

865
00:49:14,600 --> 00:49:17,120
was tell someone on the staff,
I don't remember who,

866
00:49:17,640 --> 00:49:24,560
to call the French prison authorities

867
00:49:24,640 --> 00:49:26,680
and ask them
to facilitate that conversation.

868
00:49:26,760 --> 00:49:29,000
The prison director told me

869
00:49:29,080 --> 00:49:32,200
that he had been informed I was coming

870
00:49:32,280 --> 00:49:35,120
and that I should go with him,
and he took me to a room.

871
00:49:37,760 --> 00:49:42,680
And a moment later,
an officer came back with two people.

872
00:49:42,760 --> 00:49:45,440
One of them
was José Luis Álvarez Santacristina,

873
00:49:45,520 --> 00:49:46,720
alias Txelis,

874
00:49:46,800 --> 00:49:50,000
and the other was Kepa Pikabea.

875
00:49:50,520 --> 00:49:53,000
Both were prominent ETA members.

876
00:49:53,080 --> 00:49:56,720
After saying "Hello" and "How are you?"

877
00:49:57,920 --> 00:50:03,240
the next thing I said was that I'd come
to see if there was any way to stop this

878
00:50:04,240 --> 00:50:06,040
and what we could do.

879
00:50:08,440 --> 00:50:11,120
They told me there was nothing to be done.

880
00:50:11,200 --> 00:50:14,680
The commando holding Miguel Ángel Blanco

881
00:50:15,200 --> 00:50:20,240
was waiting for news on the radio.

882
00:50:20,760 --> 00:50:24,320
Not television. Radio was much faster.

883
00:50:24,880 --> 00:50:31,000
They were waiting for the radio to say,
"They have started moving prisoners."

884
00:50:31,080 --> 00:50:32,200
That would've been enough.

885
00:50:32,280 --> 00:50:33,600
And I think they said,

886
00:50:33,680 --> 00:50:37,600
"Well, there must be seven prisoners
at the Burgos prison, which is nearby."

887
00:50:37,680 --> 00:50:41,000
"So if they just move those,
that would be enough."

888
00:50:41,080 --> 00:50:43,600
I passed that on, and that was it.

889
00:50:43,680 --> 00:50:46,800
That effort confirmed my assessment.

890
00:50:46,880 --> 00:50:52,800
The ETA member explained it
very clearly to María José Gurrutxaga.

891
00:50:53,520 --> 00:50:57,320
When commandos kidnap someone,
they seal themselves off.

892
00:50:57,840 --> 00:50:59,240
They shut themselves in.

893
00:50:59,320 --> 00:51:04,200
And if there is a 48-hour deadline,
all the more so.

894
00:51:04,280 --> 00:51:05,760
I did not…

895
00:51:07,480 --> 00:51:09,160
go into details.

896
00:51:12,040 --> 00:51:17,480
I simply gave the instruction
to do whatever was necessary,

897
00:51:19,520 --> 00:51:23,600
knowing the people
who received it would do it.

898
00:51:23,680 --> 00:51:27,760
And if any of those efforts
produced results

899
00:51:27,840 --> 00:51:30,600
that I needed to know about,
they would tell me.

900
00:51:30,680 --> 00:51:32,760
But I did not ask for details.

901
00:51:33,480 --> 00:51:35,040
So I don't know

902
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:37,440
about what you're telling me.

903
00:51:38,680 --> 00:51:41,760
But if you ask me,
"Is it possible they did it?" Yes,

904
00:51:41,840 --> 00:51:42,920
but I don't know.

905
00:51:43,520 --> 00:51:46,680
And then I left. I said goodbye to them
and left the prison.

906
00:51:46,760 --> 00:51:50,120
I felt awful.
Honestly, my spirits sank completely.

907
00:51:50,200 --> 00:51:52,600
And I thought,
"My God, what has happened?"

908
00:51:52,680 --> 00:51:55,720
"They probably didn't do anything,
and this poor guy is dead."

909
00:51:55,800 --> 00:51:59,880
That is truly what I felt in my heart.

910
00:51:59,960 --> 00:52:02,640
All that running around,
all that effort, for nothing.

911
00:52:04,560 --> 00:52:07,400
-That commando had killed many people.
-Many.

912
00:52:07,480 --> 00:52:10,360
But we know that,
despite being called the Donosti commando,

913
00:52:10,440 --> 00:52:14,120
they did not live or hide in Donosti.

914
00:52:14,200 --> 00:52:16,720
-No. Of course.
-They moved around an area…

915
00:52:16,800 --> 00:52:20,840
-Exactly.
-…between Hernani, Lasarte, Andoain…

916
00:52:20,920 --> 00:52:22,560
It is all very close.

917
00:52:22,640 --> 00:52:27,360
That was our other line of thinking.
"Let's see."

918
00:52:27,440 --> 00:52:29,280
"The Eibar area is one possibility,

919
00:52:29,360 --> 00:52:36,280
but this threat
points more toward an execution commando."

920
00:52:36,360 --> 00:52:39,400
"If we head into the mountains,

921
00:52:39,480 --> 00:52:43,160
will we be lucky enough
for them to release him in this area?"

922
00:52:43,240 --> 00:52:45,280
"He must be around here, somewhere."

923
00:52:46,680 --> 00:52:48,200
-You were more optimistic.
-Yes.

924
00:52:48,280 --> 00:52:51,600
By then, I no longer thought
they would release him.

925
00:52:51,680 --> 00:52:54,640
You were more optimistic,
I remember that well.

926
00:52:56,640 --> 00:53:01,160
As we reached the deadline, our only hope

927
00:53:02,240 --> 00:53:05,400
was to somehow run into the terrorists.

928
00:53:05,480 --> 00:53:07,120
And we were counting on the fact

929
00:53:07,200 --> 00:53:11,720
that ETA would want to kill him,
execute him,

930
00:53:11,800 --> 00:53:14,520
away from wherever they were holding him.

931
00:53:14,600 --> 00:53:18,040
So we were hoping to get lucky

932
00:53:18,120 --> 00:53:23,400
during that five-minute,
ten-minute, or half-hour drive,

933
00:53:23,480 --> 00:53:27,480
that a Civil Guard officer
would stop the car

934
00:53:27,560 --> 00:53:31,760
before it reached its destination.

935
00:53:32,400 --> 00:53:39,120
The chance to stop
Miguel Ángel's journey to his death.

936
00:53:47,000 --> 00:53:50,040
We could see that
the inevitable was about to happen.

937
00:53:50,120 --> 00:53:52,240
I admit we were devastated.

938
00:53:56,000 --> 00:53:58,440
And it was 2:00 p.m., 2:30 p.m.,

939
00:53:58,520 --> 00:54:01,400
3:00 p.m., 3:30 p.m….

940
00:54:02,960 --> 00:54:04,280
4:00 p.m.

941
00:54:12,080 --> 00:54:14,160
The bells of Ermua rang.

942
00:54:14,240 --> 00:54:17,720
No one said a word,
but you could hear the bells.

943
00:54:17,800 --> 00:54:19,760
SATURDAY
4:00 P.M.

944
00:54:19,840 --> 00:54:22,000
DEADLINE EXPIRES

945
00:54:23,560 --> 00:54:25,640
And I remember that phrase.

946
00:54:25,720 --> 00:54:28,560
She said to her, "Auntie, eat.
You haven't eaten in days."

947
00:54:28,640 --> 00:54:33,360
And my mother's answer at 4:00 p.m…
It still gives me chills.

948
00:54:33,440 --> 00:54:37,360
{\an8}"How can I eat
when they are killing my son right now?"

949
00:54:45,600 --> 00:54:47,560
We want him alive!

950
00:54:50,520 --> 00:54:53,440
I am Miguel Ángel!

951
00:54:53,520 --> 00:55:00,360
I am Miguel Ángel!

952
00:55:00,440 --> 00:55:04,040
<i>Over to our news colleagues</i>

953
00:55:04,120 --> 00:55:06,160
<i>for the latest updates.</i>

954
00:55:06,240 --> 00:55:07,840
It was 4:00 in the afternoon…

955
00:55:07,920 --> 00:55:09,160
{\an8}PHOTOGRAPHER AT EL DIARIO VASCO

956
00:55:09,240 --> 00:55:12,360
{\an8}…and I ran out because, at that moment,

957
00:55:14,000 --> 00:55:17,000
an ambulance went past us,

958
00:55:17,080 --> 00:55:21,760
and a light bulb went off in my head.
I said, "Damn, what if this is it?"

959
00:55:21,840 --> 00:55:26,480
I grabbed my camera
and followed the ambulance.

960
00:55:26,560 --> 00:55:28,800
I knew I was heading into something awful.

961
00:55:30,520 --> 00:55:32,080
And I was the first one there.

962
00:55:33,280 --> 00:55:35,520
I had to take pictures,

963
00:55:35,600 --> 00:55:38,240
and I knew
I was going to find something bad.

964
00:55:55,160 --> 00:55:57,600
Time passed, and the phone rang.

965
00:55:57,680 --> 00:56:02,280
It must have been 4:00 or 4:30 p.m.
The house phone rang.

966
00:56:04,080 --> 00:56:08,040
Obviously, the Ertzaintza officers
were the ones who answered it.

967
00:56:08,120 --> 00:56:11,520
There was a puddle,
some blood on the ground,

968
00:56:11,600 --> 00:56:13,000
and I took pictures of it.

969
00:56:13,080 --> 00:56:16,080
I took those pictures,
but I didn't like it one bit.

970
00:56:16,160 --> 00:56:18,160
I didn't like it.

971
00:56:18,240 --> 00:56:20,960
We were all gathered around the call.

972
00:56:21,720 --> 00:56:24,120
And suddenly…
We could hear half the conversation.

973
00:56:24,200 --> 00:56:28,960
"Yes. Yes. Good. Okay."

974
00:56:29,040 --> 00:56:32,480
We all drew conclusions from the half
of the conversation we could hear,

975
00:56:32,560 --> 00:56:34,080
and they sounded encouraging.

976
00:56:34,160 --> 00:56:37,920
I heard the voices
of two of the neighbors.

977
00:56:38,000 --> 00:56:40,920
"He was alive!" they said.
"He was alive! We…"

978
00:56:41,000 --> 00:56:44,840
And I said, "Was it you?"
"Yes, we found him."

979
00:56:44,920 --> 00:56:48,960
"We don't know who he is."

980
00:56:49,040 --> 00:56:52,600
And I said,
"Damn, I think it is Miguel Ángel Blanco."

981
00:56:58,160 --> 00:57:00,680
Then this Ertzaintza officer

982
00:57:00,760 --> 00:57:04,400
hung up the phone
and had to give us the news.

983
00:57:05,280 --> 00:57:07,640
She had to give us the news,
and we were all…

984
00:57:08,360 --> 00:57:12,200
"Well, he's wounded, but he's okay."

985
00:57:12,720 --> 00:57:14,800
You can imagine how we jumped for joy.

986
00:57:14,880 --> 00:57:19,240
"They have rescued him, and he is
on his way to Aránzazu Hospital."

987
00:57:19,320 --> 00:57:24,080
EMERGENCY SERVICES

988
00:57:24,600 --> 00:57:27,800
What came through on a Red Cross scanner

989
00:57:27,880 --> 00:57:31,560
was that someone
had been found near Lasarte-Oria

990
00:57:31,640 --> 00:57:34,120
with two gunshot wounds,
but both in the legs.

991
00:57:34,640 --> 00:57:37,120
{\an8}A colleague from the ER
called me and said…

992
00:57:37,200 --> 00:57:38,760
{\an8}ICU DOCTOR AT ARÁNZAZU HOSPITAL

993
00:57:38,840 --> 00:57:42,720
{\an8}"Patxi, they've found Miguel Ángel.
And he's alive."

994
00:57:43,520 --> 00:57:45,440
Well, it was a huge surprise.

995
00:57:45,520 --> 00:57:48,560
We were used to ETA's efficiency.

996
00:57:48,640 --> 00:57:49,920
ETA didn't make idle threats.

997
00:57:50,000 --> 00:57:52,440
It generally carried out its executions.

998
00:57:52,520 --> 00:57:55,240
What we first told the family

999
00:57:55,320 --> 00:57:58,680
was that we had found him,
and that his life was not in danger.

1000
00:57:58,760 --> 00:58:01,680
Two minutes later, he said,
"Patxi, bad news."

1001
00:58:01,760 --> 00:58:03,160
"They found him

1002
00:58:03,240 --> 00:58:08,120
with a gunshot wound to the head.
He's intubated, he's in a deep coma…"

1003
00:58:08,200 --> 00:58:10,560
{\an8}I headed to Aránzazu Hospital,

1004
00:58:10,640 --> 00:58:13,720
{\an8}where the victim was being taken.

1005
00:58:13,800 --> 00:58:15,640
They got him out of the ambulance.

1006
00:58:15,720 --> 00:58:17,520
I was trying to make sure,

1007
00:58:17,600 --> 00:58:21,760
because one of my priorities
was to confirm that it was him.

1008
00:58:21,840 --> 00:58:24,440
From the images I had seen,

1009
00:58:24,520 --> 00:58:26,680
I was able to confirm it.

1010
00:58:26,760 --> 00:58:29,520
{\an8}I went to the ER
to wait for the ambulance…

1011
00:58:29,600 --> 00:58:30,720
{\an8}ARÁNZAZU HOSPITAL CHAPLAIN

1012
00:58:30,800 --> 00:58:34,960
{\an8}…and as soon as it arrived, I gave him
absolution right there at the ER entrance.

1013
00:58:35,040 --> 00:58:39,280
It was not the time
to interrupt the medical team's work,

1014
00:58:39,360 --> 00:58:42,600
but at least
I could absolve him of his sins.

1015
00:58:50,560 --> 00:58:52,880
<i>Reports from the scene are conflicting.</i>

1016
00:58:55,320 --> 00:58:58,160
<i>Is he alive? Is he gravely wounded?</i>

1017
00:58:58,240 --> 00:58:59,520
<i>Is he dead?</i>

1018
00:59:00,040 --> 00:59:02,040
{\an8}-Be quiet, please!
-What happened?

1019
00:59:02,120 --> 00:59:08,480
{\an8}<i>…that was the news coming</i>
<i>from our colleague Luján in San Sebastián…</i>

1020
00:59:08,560 --> 00:59:10,600
-So he's dead.
-Yes.

1021
00:59:10,680 --> 00:59:13,960
<i>…with a gunshot wound to the head,</i>
<i>and he has been taken to the hospital…</i>

1022
00:59:14,040 --> 00:59:16,320
Someone turned up
with a gunshot wound to the head.

1023
00:59:16,400 --> 00:59:18,920
It has not been confirmed yet,
but it is probably him.

1024
00:59:19,000 --> 00:59:21,200
-From the description, it sounds like him.
-Yes.

1025
00:59:21,280 --> 00:59:25,160
<i>If they had wanted to release him,</i>
<i>they would have released him alive,</i>

1026
00:59:25,240 --> 00:59:28,160
<i>not after shooting him. So it seems…</i>

1027
00:59:28,240 --> 00:59:32,480
<i>…the monstrous act of brutality</i>
<i>ETA has just committed.</i>

1028
00:59:32,560 --> 00:59:36,640
<i>The appeals from Herri Batasuna</i>
<i>and Patxi Zabaleta have been useless…</i>

1029
00:59:36,720 --> 00:59:37,920
He's still alive!

1030
00:59:38,000 --> 00:59:39,920
-He's alive?
-He's still alive!

1031
00:59:40,000 --> 00:59:46,400
Don't die, Miguel Ángel!

1032
00:59:46,480 --> 00:59:50,720
When the family came down from the house
to go to Aránzazu Hospital,

1033
00:59:50,800 --> 00:59:54,520
they looked faintly relieved,

1034
00:59:54,600 --> 00:59:56,320
Chelo was even smiling.

1035
00:59:57,920 --> 00:59:59,840
Murderers! Cowards!

1036
01:00:00,640 --> 01:00:01,680
Murderers!

1037
01:00:02,280 --> 01:00:05,880
{\an8}I remember coming down,
and people shouting, "Murderers!"

1038
01:00:05,960 --> 01:00:08,200
{\an8}And I remember telling them,

1039
01:00:08,280 --> 01:00:11,960
"No, he's okay, he's alive.
It's a superficial wound."

1040
01:00:12,480 --> 01:00:14,080
We got into that car,

1041
01:00:14,160 --> 01:00:17,080
and for me,
it was the happiest journey of my life.

1042
01:00:17,160 --> 01:00:19,520
{\an8}People here have been left speechless.

1043
01:00:19,600 --> 01:00:21,840
{\an8}They are in utter shock.

1044
01:00:21,920 --> 01:00:24,080
That is what you can see here.

1045
01:00:24,160 --> 01:00:27,400
{\an8}Even members of the press are overcome.

1046
01:00:27,480 --> 01:00:28,640
{\an8}LIVE
BLANCO FAMILY HOME

1047
01:00:28,720 --> 01:00:30,320
{\an8}I can't say anything else. Goodbye.

1048
01:00:30,400 --> 01:00:35,760
{\an8}I looked at the people
standing opposite us.

1049
01:00:35,840 --> 01:00:37,760
I noticed some of the journalists.

1050
01:00:37,840 --> 01:00:40,640
I remember, for example, Verónica's face,

1051
01:00:40,720 --> 01:00:42,760
the woman from Televisión Española,

1052
01:00:42,840 --> 01:00:45,960
and how, as the reports kept changing,

1053
01:00:46,040 --> 01:00:48,120
first saying he had been shot

1054
01:00:48,200 --> 01:00:50,800
but did not seem badly wounded,
then that he was…

1055
01:00:50,880 --> 01:00:52,400
I saw Verónica crying,

1056
01:00:52,480 --> 01:00:56,240
and then we got in the car
and set off for San Sebastián.

1057
01:00:56,320 --> 01:00:59,000
I was speaking,
and there came a point when I thought,

1058
01:00:59,080 --> 01:01:01,800
"If you say three more words,

1059
01:01:02,920 --> 01:01:06,080
you are going to burst into tears."

1060
01:01:08,760 --> 01:01:11,080
Murderers!

1061
01:01:19,400 --> 01:01:21,360
When I was informed

1062
01:01:21,440 --> 01:01:23,560
that Miguel Ángel Blanco had been found,

1063
01:01:23,640 --> 01:01:28,040
that he had been shot twice
in the head and was, I thought, dead,

1064
01:01:28,120 --> 01:01:30,400
without thinking it through,

1065
01:01:30,480 --> 01:01:33,920
I knew I had to go out
onto the balcony and tell the residents.

1066
01:01:34,000 --> 01:01:37,080
No script, no notes.

1067
01:01:37,160 --> 01:01:39,480
Miguel Ángel has been murdered.

1068
01:01:43,880 --> 01:01:47,160
Precisely so that Miguel's death
can mean something,

1069
01:01:47,240 --> 01:01:49,400
we must demonstrate peacefully.

1070
01:01:49,480 --> 01:01:51,080
I stayed at the house.

1071
01:01:52,160 --> 01:01:56,520
After the 48 hours, I said,
"Well…" I even went into Miguel's room.

1072
01:01:56,600 --> 01:02:00,800
And the same Ertzaintza unit
that had given us that news

1073
01:02:00,880 --> 01:02:03,760
said to me,
"We have some not-so-good news."

1074
01:02:10,480 --> 01:02:12,160
Our first impression

1075
01:02:12,240 --> 01:02:16,560
in radiology was that
there was nothing we could do.

1076
01:02:17,360 --> 01:02:21,920
But doctors always say
we do not have all the answers

1077
01:02:22,000 --> 01:02:25,960
and that there is always hope as long
as there is a flicker of life.

1078
01:02:26,560 --> 01:02:28,320
And in this particular case,

1079
01:02:28,400 --> 01:02:32,240
although we thought he was brain-dead,

1080
01:02:32,760 --> 01:02:35,400
there was still
some spontaneous breathing.

1081
01:02:35,480 --> 01:02:38,280
NUESTRA SEÑORA DE ARÁNZAZU HOSPITAL

1082
01:02:40,640 --> 01:02:44,880
The shock came
when we entered the hospital.

1083
01:02:44,960 --> 01:02:48,320
The looks on people's faces
told us something was wrong.

1084
01:02:48,400 --> 01:02:50,960
And when the doctor saw us,

1085
01:02:51,040 --> 01:02:53,240
he told us it was not a superficial wound,

1086
01:02:53,320 --> 01:02:57,720
that he had been hit twice in the head

1087
01:02:57,800 --> 01:03:01,400
and that his life was hanging by a thread.

1088
01:03:02,200 --> 01:03:04,520
We went from happiness

1089
01:03:05,200 --> 01:03:08,560
back to unbearable pain.

1090
01:03:11,360 --> 01:03:12,360
Come on!

1091
01:03:13,000 --> 01:03:15,560
-Everyone be quiet!
-Quiet!

1092
01:03:15,640 --> 01:03:17,000
I remember it perfectly

1093
01:03:17,080 --> 01:03:21,160
because I had the impression
there was a volcano in the street.

1094
01:03:21,240 --> 01:03:26,240
The street was completely filled
with thousands and thousands of people

1095
01:03:26,320 --> 01:03:30,520
radiating anguish, rage, and grief

1096
01:03:30,600 --> 01:03:33,000
in a way that I sensed was dangerous.

1097
01:03:33,080 --> 01:03:37,720
It seemed to me that the announcement
could lead to violence,

1098
01:03:37,800 --> 01:03:40,960
and that is why I felt
we had to go down into the street

1099
01:03:41,040 --> 01:03:44,320
and start that march toward Eibar,
to calm people down,

1100
01:03:44,400 --> 01:03:47,280
before things got violent and out of hand.

1101
01:03:47,360 --> 01:03:51,000
It was incredible. People were screaming,

1102
01:03:51,080 --> 01:03:54,440
and there was so much sadness.

1103
01:03:54,520 --> 01:03:57,280
I don't think anyone
could keep from crying,

1104
01:03:57,360 --> 01:04:01,480
with all the grief, rage,
and anger we were feeling.

1105
01:04:02,280 --> 01:04:03,640
The image was devastating.

1106
01:04:03,720 --> 01:04:09,000
People shouting, crying,
lying on the ground, kicking, hitting…

1107
01:04:09,760 --> 01:04:14,360
I remember seeing him with a black eye,
and I thought he had been tortured.

1108
01:04:14,440 --> 01:04:19,680
The doctor reassured me and said,
"That is from the bullets' impact."

1109
01:04:19,760 --> 01:04:23,680
Those hours at the hospital
were very, very hard.

1110
01:04:23,760 --> 01:04:27,080
To us, he looked like he was asleep.

1111
01:04:27,680 --> 01:04:30,400
A mother generally wants to see her child.

1112
01:04:30,480 --> 01:04:33,040
She hadn't seen him
in three agonizing days.

1113
01:04:33,120 --> 01:04:35,360
I went up and said,
"Do you want to come see him?"

1114
01:04:35,440 --> 01:04:38,800
She calmed down as soon as I asked.

1115
01:04:38,880 --> 01:04:44,000
She went in and touched her son.
"Miguel Ángel, I'm here, sweetheart."

1116
01:04:45,840 --> 01:04:48,400
It has been confirmed
that Miguel Ángel Blanco is still alive.

1117
01:04:48,480 --> 01:04:49,840
He is undergoing surgery

1118
01:04:49,920 --> 01:04:52,160
at Aránzazu Hospital in San Sebastián,

1119
01:04:52,240 --> 01:04:54,320
and, although his condition
is very serious,

1120
01:04:54,400 --> 01:04:57,120
his vital signs remain stable.

1121
01:05:02,160 --> 01:05:05,240
There are sacrifices you accept

1122
01:05:05,320 --> 01:05:07,320
because they are tied to your convictions,

1123
01:05:07,400 --> 01:05:11,400
and others, like this one,
that are forced upon you.

1124
01:05:12,640 --> 01:05:14,080
We tried to prepare them.

1125
01:05:14,160 --> 01:05:18,120
We kept updating them on his condition.

1126
01:05:18,200 --> 01:05:19,680
Every hour or… I don't remember.

1127
01:05:19,760 --> 01:05:22,280
Every so often,
I went out and updated them.

1128
01:05:22,880 --> 01:05:25,720
At that point, honestly,
my faith did not help me either.

1129
01:05:25,800 --> 01:05:27,600
I got angry.

1130
01:05:28,240 --> 01:05:29,920
I rebelled. I asked why.

1131
01:05:30,000 --> 01:05:33,200
Why my brother, who was so good,
such a believer…? Why?

1132
01:05:33,280 --> 01:05:35,960
It was horrible. I think my mind erased
the hospital from my memory,

1133
01:05:36,040 --> 01:05:39,160
as if to say,
"This won't let you move forward."

1134
01:05:39,240 --> 01:05:40,720
Those were very hard moments.

1135
01:05:40,800 --> 01:05:45,080
Later, once things were calmer,

1136
01:05:45,160 --> 01:05:47,640
I was able to give him
the anointing of the sick in the ICU.

1137
01:05:47,720 --> 01:05:50,640
In the past,
it was called "extreme unction."

1138
01:05:51,160 --> 01:05:52,600
…outside the brain,

1139
01:05:53,320 --> 01:05:56,560
there's a fragment here.

1140
01:05:56,640 --> 01:05:58,720
A small piece of the bullet.

1141
01:05:59,640 --> 01:06:02,600
And the other one
is lodged in the frontal area.

1142
01:06:02,680 --> 01:06:07,160
You can see the path.
This is the one that affected the brain.

1143
01:06:07,240 --> 01:06:08,800
There were many visitors,

1144
01:06:09,680 --> 01:06:11,960
and perhaps the one
that affected me the most

1145
01:06:12,040 --> 01:06:13,560
was Mayor Oreja,

1146
01:06:13,640 --> 01:06:16,120
because he was
interior minister at the time.

1147
01:06:16,200 --> 01:06:20,000
At one point, he said to me,
"Doctor, is there somewhere

1148
01:06:20,080 --> 01:06:23,480
where I can
speak confidentially with the family?"

1149
01:06:23,560 --> 01:06:27,480
When they came out of that meeting,

1150
01:06:27,560 --> 01:06:28,840
they seemed calmer.

1151
01:06:28,920 --> 01:06:31,520
I hugged them,

1152
01:06:32,040 --> 01:06:37,280
looked after them,
encouraged them as much as I could,

1153
01:06:37,360 --> 01:06:40,840
but they did not even
ask me for an explanation.

1154
01:06:52,280 --> 01:06:53,480
I will always remember

1155
01:06:53,560 --> 01:06:57,840
how Totorika organized a march
to tire everyone out,

1156
01:06:57,920 --> 01:07:02,440
so that physical and emotional exhaustion

1157
01:07:02,520 --> 01:07:07,160
would prevent any unwanted
or harmful reactions.

1158
01:07:09,080 --> 01:07:14,840
He led us all on foot to Eibar,
two or three miles away.

1159
01:07:15,360 --> 01:07:18,680
And I don't know how…
Honestly, I don't know how he did it,

1160
01:07:18,760 --> 01:07:20,800
but he led us behind him like sheep.

1161
01:07:35,680 --> 01:07:41,000
{\an8}I got married on July 12, 1997.

1162
01:07:41,080 --> 01:07:42,640
{\an8}ERMUA RESIDENT

1163
01:07:42,720 --> 01:07:47,800
I had planned the whole wedding
with such excitement.

1164
01:07:47,880 --> 01:07:53,280
We went to the restaurant
to try to keep celebrating,

1165
01:07:53,360 --> 01:07:55,760
but we weren't really celebrating.

1166
01:07:55,840 --> 01:08:00,760
We made the restaurant owner turn on
the TV so we could follow the news.

1167
01:08:00,840 --> 01:08:02,760
We wanted to know what was happening.

1168
01:08:02,840 --> 01:08:05,400
I remember locking myself
in the bathroom with my cousin

1169
01:08:05,480 --> 01:08:08,440
and crying from all the anguish we felt.

1170
01:08:08,520 --> 01:08:10,880
I was not happy that day.

1171
01:08:10,960 --> 01:08:14,560
I cannot say
that was the happiest day of my life.

1172
01:08:14,640 --> 01:08:15,800
It was not.

1173
01:08:16,520 --> 01:08:18,800
And then the march passed

1174
01:08:18,880 --> 01:08:22,640
right below where we were celebrating,

1175
01:08:22,720 --> 01:08:24,040
and I decided…

1176
01:08:25,560 --> 01:08:29,640
My relatives and I all joined the march

1177
01:08:29,720 --> 01:08:32,680
and walked with it all the way to Eibar.

1178
01:08:32,760 --> 01:08:37,800
Me in my wedding dress,
with my shoes in my hand…

1179
01:08:37,880 --> 01:08:40,320
And we walked down to Eibar.
I did not celebrate.

1180
01:08:40,400 --> 01:08:42,400
I never got to celebrate anything.

1181
01:08:42,480 --> 01:08:44,760
{\an8}UNZAGA SQUARE

1182
01:08:44,840 --> 01:08:47,040
{\an8}<i>At that hour, Miguel was still alive.</i>

1183
01:08:48,040 --> 01:08:50,680
<i>For many, there was hope again.</i>

1184
01:08:50,760 --> 01:08:52,680
{\an8}STATEMENT BY CARLOS TOTORIKA
MAYOR OF ERMUA, PSOE

1185
01:08:52,760 --> 01:08:54,160
{\an8}Residents of Ermua and Eibar,

1186
01:08:55,160 --> 01:08:58,920
first of all, I truly want to thank you

1187
01:08:59,440 --> 01:09:01,440
on behalf of Miguel Ángel's family

1188
01:09:02,240 --> 01:09:05,040
for the support
they have received from all of you.

1189
01:09:05,960 --> 01:09:09,400
{\an8}MAYOR OF EIBAR, PSOE

1190
01:09:09,480 --> 01:09:12,440
Today, Miguel Ángel is still alive.

1191
01:09:12,520 --> 01:09:14,080
There is hope.

1192
01:09:14,160 --> 01:09:16,680
We must hold on to that hope,
keep it alive,

1193
01:09:16,760 --> 01:09:20,480
and hopefully we will be able
to celebrate his return home.

1194
01:09:24,720 --> 01:09:28,200
{\an8}<i>The hours pass,</i>
<i>and in San Sebastián, a protest</i>

1195
01:09:28,280 --> 01:09:31,920
{\an8}<i>outside the headquarters</i>
<i>of Herri Batasuna, ETA's political wing,</i>

1196
01:09:32,000 --> 01:09:33,440
{\an8}<i>is about to explode.</i>

1197
01:09:34,720 --> 01:09:38,400
<i>The Basque police</i>
<i>guarded the entrance to prevent lynchings.</i>

1198
01:09:38,920 --> 01:09:42,640
<i>Over the course of its history,</i>
<i>ETA murdered 15 members of the Ertzaintza.</i>

1199
01:09:42,720 --> 01:09:47,080
<i>That is why they wore masks,</i>
<i>to avoid being recognized and threatened.</i>

1200
01:09:47,160 --> 01:09:48,960
<i>The protesters could not understand</i>

1201
01:09:49,040 --> 01:09:51,960
<i>why the police were protecting</i>
<i>the people who killed them.</i>

1202
01:09:52,040 --> 01:09:53,680
<i>The situation was chaotic.</i>

1203
01:09:53,760 --> 01:09:55,960
<i>The commanding officer, Imanol Rodríguez,</i>

1204
01:09:56,040 --> 01:09:59,200
<i>took a risk and managed</i>
<i>to contain all that rage</i>

1205
01:09:59,280 --> 01:10:01,680
<i>with an action</i>
<i>that has gone down in history.</i>

1206
01:10:01,760 --> 01:10:04,800
We felt supported.

1207
01:10:06,720 --> 01:10:09,200
{\an8}My colleague, to my left, said to me,

1208
01:10:09,280 --> 01:10:12,040
{\an8}"How about we take off our hoods?"

1209
01:10:12,680 --> 01:10:13,680
I was in charge.

1210
01:10:14,360 --> 01:10:15,360
I took off my helmet,

1211
01:10:15,440 --> 01:10:19,360
my colleagues took off
their hoods and helmets,

1212
01:10:19,440 --> 01:10:23,280
and suddenly, a wave of people
came over and hugged us.

1213
01:10:23,360 --> 01:10:26,880
We did not realize
how important it would later become,

1214
01:10:26,960 --> 01:10:29,960
because I thought, "I'm going to be
in serious trouble for this."

1215
01:10:30,040 --> 01:10:32,880
Otegi to the firing squad!

1216
01:10:32,960 --> 01:10:35,040
Ertzaintza!

1217
01:10:35,120 --> 01:10:38,720
For the Ertzaintza,
showing their faces in public,

1218
01:10:38,800 --> 01:10:43,040
{\an8}even in front of the press,
was a milestone.

1219
01:10:43,120 --> 01:10:45,240
{\an8}It was not just
those people we were hiding from.

1220
01:10:45,320 --> 01:10:48,280
Anyone could identify us
as Ertzaintza officers.

1221
01:10:48,360 --> 01:10:49,560
We overcame our fear.

1222
01:10:49,640 --> 01:10:51,520
{\an8}HERRI BATASUNA
ERMUA HEADQUARTERS

1223
01:10:51,600 --> 01:10:55,520
{\an8}Well, the Herri Batasuna headquarters
was set on fire.

1224
01:10:55,600 --> 01:10:57,760
I grabbed a fire extinguisher
with one of them

1225
01:10:57,840 --> 01:10:59,400
and went to put the fire out.

1226
01:11:05,240 --> 01:11:10,320
Around 1:00 a.m.,
I wanted to go see Miguel Ángel Blanco.

1227
01:11:11,040 --> 01:11:16,160
I went to the hospital and saw him
through a glass window, lying there.

1228
01:11:17,040 --> 01:11:19,800
They had not yet declared him dead.

1229
01:11:19,880 --> 01:11:24,240
And from deep inside me came the thought,

1230
01:11:24,760 --> 01:11:28,880
"I'm so sorry we couldn't
do anything to stop it."

1231
01:11:28,960 --> 01:11:31,160
He was no longer breathing,

1232
01:11:31,240 --> 01:11:34,600
and that was when we decided to do an EEG.

1233
01:11:35,200 --> 01:11:37,720
The test runs for at least half an hour.

1234
01:11:37,800 --> 01:11:40,680
And during that half hour,
there was no brain activity.

1235
01:11:41,360 --> 01:11:45,160
When they told his father
they were going to turn off the machine,

1236
01:11:45,240 --> 01:11:48,720
he asked if he could see him first,

1237
01:11:48,800 --> 01:11:51,680
to be with Miguel in his final moments.

1238
01:11:52,400 --> 01:11:56,520
At 3:00 a.m., they called me to the ICU

1239
01:11:56,600 --> 01:12:00,840
and asked me to help tell his mother

1240
01:12:00,920 --> 01:12:06,240
that her beloved son,
the center of her world, had died.

1241
01:12:06,320 --> 01:12:10,000
That the body she saw breathing
was doing so mechanically,

1242
01:12:10,080 --> 01:12:13,160
but that he was already gone.

1243
01:12:13,240 --> 01:12:15,920
And I will never forget
the image of Chelo,

1244
01:12:16,640 --> 01:12:17,720
completely dazed,

1245
01:12:17,800 --> 01:12:21,440
her feet dragging
along the fourth-floor corridor.

1246
01:12:21,520 --> 01:12:24,120
We ran another EEG,

1247
01:12:24,200 --> 01:12:27,440
and it confirmed what we already knew.

1248
01:12:27,520 --> 01:12:30,240
His heartbeat was barely detectable.

1249
01:12:30,320 --> 01:12:33,680
It continued to slow, and he died.

1250
01:12:37,400 --> 01:12:41,680
At 3:00 a.m. on July 13th,
the neurological examination

1251
01:12:41,760 --> 01:12:45,280
of the patient, Mr. Miguel Ángel Blanco,
was consistent with brain death,

1252
01:12:45,360 --> 01:12:48,800
which was confirmed by an EEG

1253
01:12:48,880 --> 01:12:51,160
showing an isoelectric tracing,

1254
01:12:51,240 --> 01:12:55,800
commonly known as a flat EEG.

1255
01:12:55,880 --> 01:13:00,160
These findings confirm
the diagnosis of clinical brain death.

1256
01:13:08,040 --> 01:13:10,960
ETA MURDERS MIGUEL ÁNGEL
DESPITE PUBLIC OUTCRY TO SAVE HIS LIFE

1257
01:13:11,520 --> 01:13:16,240
ETA ABANDONED MIGUEL ÁNGEL BLANCO
AFTER SHOOTING HIM TWICE IN THE HEAD

1258
01:13:17,560 --> 01:13:19,080
DESOLATION

1259
01:13:19,160 --> 01:13:20,520
HOPE SHATTERED

1260
01:13:20,600 --> 01:13:21,520
A WOUNDED TOWN

1261
01:13:22,760 --> 01:13:24,560
MIGUEL ÁNGEL NOT ABLE
TO SURVIVE ETA ATTACK

1262
01:13:24,640 --> 01:13:27,800
ETA EXECUTED MIGUEL ÁNGEL,
HANDS TIED, SHOT TWICE IN THE HEAD

1263
01:13:28,640 --> 01:13:30,680
{\an8}ETA'S VILE ACT

1264
01:13:32,160 --> 01:13:35,200
{\an8}THE GOVERNMENT DID NOT BUDGE,
AND ETA SHOT THE PP COUNCILMAN

1265
01:13:35,280 --> 01:13:38,200
BEASTS!

1266
01:13:38,280 --> 01:13:40,040
{\an8}ETA DIDN'T LISTEN TO THE PEOPLE

1267
01:13:40,120 --> 01:13:43,000
{\an8}SONS OF BITCHES

1268
01:13:43,080 --> 01:13:44,960
I remember, at 8:00 a.m.,

1269
01:13:45,040 --> 01:13:47,160
and this is something I regret,

1270
01:13:47,240 --> 01:13:48,880
something that will haunt me…

1271
01:13:49,920 --> 01:13:51,240
for the rest of my life,

1272
01:13:52,880 --> 01:13:56,480
that I did not have the courage
to go down and say goodbye to my brother

1273
01:13:57,200 --> 01:13:58,240
after he had died.

1274
01:13:58,320 --> 01:13:59,960
And that is something…

1275
01:14:01,920 --> 01:14:03,640
I can't get over it,

1276
01:14:03,720 --> 01:14:06,520
because the next time I saw my brother…

1277
01:14:11,080 --> 01:14:13,240
he was in his coffin, in Ermua.

1278
01:14:16,760 --> 01:14:18,880
<i>We were all stunned.</i>

1279
01:14:18,960 --> 01:14:20,040
<i>Broken.</i>

1280
01:14:20,760 --> 01:14:24,880
<i>A collective outpouring of grief</i>
<i>united people of all ages and ideologies.</i>

1281
01:14:25,400 --> 01:14:28,440
<i>But there was something more,</i>
<i>especially in Euskadi.</i>

1282
01:14:28,520 --> 01:14:32,240
<i>The pent-up rage,</i>
<i>that spirit that emerged in Ermua,</i>

1283
01:14:32,800 --> 01:14:34,440
<i>gave rise to a new feeling.</i>

1284
01:14:35,760 --> 01:14:37,560
<i>We had lost our fear of ETA.</i>

1285
01:14:38,080 --> 01:14:41,400
There was a moment when
they threw themselves to the ground,

1286
01:14:41,920 --> 01:14:45,520
put their hands behind their heads,

1287
01:14:45,600 --> 01:14:48,720
and shouted,
"ETA, listen. Here's my neck."

1288
01:14:48,800 --> 01:14:53,000
ETA, here's my neck!

1289
01:14:53,080 --> 01:14:55,760
ETA, here's my neck!

1290
01:14:55,840 --> 01:14:59,040
I took those photos,
which are iconic to me,

1291
01:14:59,120 --> 01:15:03,240
and I could see the shouting…

1292
01:15:03,320 --> 01:15:04,920
ETA, here's my neck!

1293
01:15:05,000 --> 01:15:06,520
…the horror,

1294
01:15:06,600 --> 01:15:08,520
the despair.

1295
01:15:09,840 --> 01:15:13,440
{\an8}I was silent for quite a while.

1296
01:15:13,520 --> 01:15:14,480
{\an8}PHOTOGRAPHER

1297
01:15:14,560 --> 01:15:17,440
I stepped away for a while

1298
01:15:18,320 --> 01:15:24,600
and did not feel comfortable
with what I was experiencing.

1299
01:15:25,200 --> 01:15:26,600
That day,

1300
01:15:27,320 --> 01:15:29,720
I saw seasoned photographers

1301
01:15:30,240 --> 01:15:34,040
lower their cameras because
they could not contain their emotion.

1302
01:15:34,720 --> 01:15:37,720
Colleagues stopped reporting

1303
01:15:37,800 --> 01:15:42,280
because the sound was bouncing
off the walls and was almost deafening.

1304
01:15:42,880 --> 01:15:46,040
There was a moment at the town hall,
when they brought Miguel in…

1305
01:15:46,120 --> 01:15:47,880
And it happened to all of us.

1306
01:15:49,320 --> 01:15:53,600
{\an8}I did not believe anything had happened
to Miguel until I saw him. Truly.

1307
01:15:53,680 --> 01:15:54,880
{\an8}WAKE AT THE TOWN HALL

1308
01:15:54,960 --> 01:15:57,600
{\an8}And instinctively, we all rushed over

1309
01:15:57,680 --> 01:16:00,080
to look through the little window
they had left uncovered,

1310
01:16:00,160 --> 01:16:01,240
to see if it was him.

1311
01:16:01,760 --> 01:16:04,040
Until I saw him there,

1312
01:16:04,120 --> 01:16:08,040
my mind simply would not accept it.
It was as if to say, "This can't be."

1313
01:16:25,240 --> 01:16:28,040
Going to Ermua was not an easy decision.

1314
01:16:29,680 --> 01:16:35,040
Terrorism was still very active,
and a murder had just taken place.

1315
01:16:35,560 --> 01:16:37,520
Security was a big concern.

1316
01:16:39,040 --> 01:16:45,080
And it had been agreed that the king,
the head of state, would not travel there.

1317
01:16:45,160 --> 01:16:48,560
But how could we reconcile that need

1318
01:16:49,680 --> 01:16:54,280
with the Crown's desire to share not just
its condolences with Spanish society,

1319
01:16:54,360 --> 01:16:57,000
but also a more direct
and forceful message

1320
01:16:57,080 --> 01:17:02,640
echoing that collective
"enough is enough" to terrorism?

1321
01:17:02,720 --> 01:17:05,720
So the king and the prime minister

1322
01:17:05,800 --> 01:17:11,080
agreed that the right decision
was for me to go.

1323
01:17:20,800 --> 01:17:25,640
And, well, it was very moving, very…
There were huge crowds in the streets.

1324
01:17:25,720 --> 01:17:28,800
They carried him
from the church to the cemetery.

1325
01:17:28,880 --> 01:17:32,600
Different groups
carried the coffin on their shoulders.

1326
01:17:32,680 --> 01:17:36,000
Miguel!

1327
01:17:36,080 --> 01:17:40,840
And we, his coworkers,
and I think some Red Cross workers,

1328
01:17:40,920 --> 01:17:43,800
{\an8}were the ones who placed
his coffin in the burial niche.

1329
01:17:43,880 --> 01:17:48,840
{\an8}ERMUA CEMETERY

1330
01:17:54,080 --> 01:17:57,840
It was not easy to say,
"Well, is my presence enough,

1331
01:17:58,360 --> 01:18:00,680
or should I say something there?"

1332
01:18:02,320 --> 01:18:05,880
It was unusual. It was the first time
I had done anything like that.

1333
01:18:06,680 --> 01:18:11,440
{\an8}I have come to Ermua
to express the royal family's support…

1334
01:18:11,520 --> 01:18:13,480
{\an8}STATEMENT BY THE PRINCE OF ASTURIAS
ERMUA CEMETERY

1335
01:18:13,560 --> 01:18:15,920
…for Basque society,
for the Basque people…

1336
01:18:16,000 --> 01:18:22,760
I felt a strong need to say something
because of that generational connection.

1337
01:18:22,840 --> 01:18:26,000
We were practically the same age,
28 or 29.

1338
01:18:26,080 --> 01:18:28,880
And, at the same time,
I wanted to convey a message

1339
01:18:28,960 --> 01:18:31,280
that was not simply one of condolence,

1340
01:18:32,000 --> 01:18:38,000
not something formal or scripted,
but something that felt close,

1341
01:18:38,080 --> 01:18:41,560
both physically and emotionally,

1342
01:18:41,640 --> 01:18:43,200
to what this represented.

1343
01:18:43,880 --> 01:18:49,200
…and its firm rejection
of this latest terrorist attack,

1344
01:18:49,880 --> 01:18:54,760
which has affected
the Blanco Garrido family so directly,

1345
01:18:55,280 --> 01:18:57,680
and has shaken us all.

1346
01:18:58,920 --> 01:19:02,440
It was a very powerful feeling.

1347
01:19:02,960 --> 01:19:04,160
Very powerful.

1348
01:19:04,680 --> 01:19:09,640
Because we were all
constantly reliving those terrible events.

1349
01:19:10,240 --> 01:19:14,720
And that sense of collective anguish

1350
01:19:14,800 --> 01:19:20,560
spread powerfully across Spain.

1351
01:19:20,640 --> 01:19:22,880
People even followed it from abroad.

1352
01:19:26,720 --> 01:19:30,320
<i>…in the case</i>
<i>of Miguel Ángel Blanco Garrido.</i>

1353
01:19:37,120 --> 01:19:38,200
From that moment on,

1354
01:19:38,280 --> 01:19:40,400
Miguel came to represent
something larger than himself.

1355
01:19:40,480 --> 01:19:42,200
Perhaps rightly so,

1356
01:19:42,280 --> 01:19:45,520
because his death
may have helped change many things.

1357
01:19:45,600 --> 01:19:50,560
And naturally, he became an archetype.

1358
01:19:50,640 --> 01:19:52,280
Not a character, but an archetype

1359
01:19:52,360 --> 01:19:56,960
onto which people
project whatever they want.

1360
01:20:12,160 --> 01:20:14,480
<i>From that July 12th on, Miguel,</i>

1361
01:20:15,000 --> 01:20:18,720
<i>the Héroes del Silencio fan</i>
<i>and drummer in the band Póker,</i>

1362
01:20:18,800 --> 01:20:22,160
<i>the young man who handled</i>
<i>your paperwork at the agency,</i>

1363
01:20:22,240 --> 01:20:26,560
<i>the young councilman who was</i>
<i>going to marry Marimar and quit politics,</i>

1364
01:20:27,520 --> 01:20:29,360
<i>stopped being Miguel</i>

1365
01:20:30,400 --> 01:20:32,760
<i>and became Miguel Ángel Blanco.</i>

1366
01:20:39,840 --> 01:20:42,760
<i>I have returned many times</i>
<i>to the place where they killed him.</i>

1367
01:20:43,280 --> 01:20:48,120
<i>I never saw flowers, a monument,</i>
<i>or a plaque commemorating him.</i>

1368
01:20:49,680 --> 01:20:52,760
<i>In the last three years,</i>
<i>someone has carved a cross</i>

1369
01:20:52,840 --> 01:20:56,040
<i>into one of the trees</i>
<i>that heard the shots.</i>

1370
01:21:00,240 --> 01:21:01,400
<i>In recent months,</i>

1371
01:21:02,000 --> 01:21:05,960
<i>someone else, or perhaps the same person,</i>
<i>has painted the cross.</i>

1372
01:21:06,880 --> 01:21:08,800
<i>They painted it white.</i>

1373
01:21:15,440 --> 01:21:18,600
<i>Of the three killers,</i>
<i>only two are still alive.</i>

1374
01:21:18,680 --> 01:21:22,000
<i>The shooter,</i>
<i>Javier García Gaztelu, "Txapote,"</i>

1375
01:21:22,080 --> 01:21:25,400
<i>and Irantzu Gallastegi Sodupe,</i>
<i>alias "Amaia."</i>

1376
01:21:25,920 --> 01:21:28,960
<i>But they have never admitted their crime.</i>

1377
01:21:29,560 --> 01:21:33,920
<i>Nor has the hideout where they kept him</i>
<i>been found, or the weapon they used.</i>

1378
01:21:34,440 --> 01:21:38,360
<i>If they have not confessed,</i>
<i>how were they convicted, then?</i>

1379
01:21:41,560 --> 01:21:43,680
{\an8}<i>Because other terrorists gave them up.</i>

1380
01:21:43,760 --> 01:21:47,640
{\an8}<i>For example,</i>
<i>the informant who hid them, Ibon Muñoa.</i>

1381
01:21:48,440 --> 01:21:52,120
The next day, after Miguel Ángel
had died at the hospital,

1382
01:21:52,200 --> 01:21:54,920
we had another
town council meeting in Eibar.

1383
01:21:55,000 --> 01:21:57,440
{\an8}And beside me, the killer sat down again,

1384
01:21:57,520 --> 01:21:58,720
{\an8}the terrorist

1385
01:21:58,800 --> 01:22:03,080
{\an8}who had passed on information
and sheltered the commando in his home.

1386
01:22:03,160 --> 01:22:04,680
He was one of the killers.

1387
01:22:10,160 --> 01:22:12,160
I identified Ibon Muñoa.

1388
01:22:13,400 --> 01:22:14,520
{\an8}I had spent years

1389
01:22:14,600 --> 01:22:16,320
{\an8}FORMER NATIONAL POLICE COMMISSIONER

1390
01:22:16,400 --> 01:22:21,800
{\an8}looking for an auto parts
and accessories shop,

1391
01:22:22,320 --> 01:22:25,200
like the one Ibon Muñoa ran,

1392
01:22:25,280 --> 01:22:29,000
where we knew
license plates were being made

1393
01:22:29,080 --> 01:22:32,000
for ETA's car bombs.

1394
01:22:35,480 --> 01:22:39,640
I drove to Eibar, went into Ibon's shop,

1395
01:22:39,720 --> 01:22:43,200
and ordered license plates for my own car.

1396
01:22:43,720 --> 01:22:47,040
The consultancy
where Miguel Ángel Blanco worked

1397
01:22:47,120 --> 01:22:51,120
was practically opposite Ibon's shop.

1398
01:22:51,200 --> 01:22:53,560
The next day, we checked them…

1399
01:22:54,080 --> 01:22:58,080
Forensics compared them under a microscope

1400
01:22:58,160 --> 01:23:01,560
and were able to determine with certainty

1401
01:23:02,080 --> 01:23:05,280
that they matched the license plates

1402
01:23:05,800 --> 01:23:09,760
we had found in the possession
of various ETA commandos.

1403
01:23:11,320 --> 01:23:12,160
In fact,

1404
01:23:12,240 --> 01:23:16,760
the three members of the Donosti commando
stayed at his house.

1405
01:23:16,840 --> 01:23:20,680
Txapote was the one who asked Ibon

1406
01:23:21,760 --> 01:23:25,880
to monitor and track Miguel Ángel Blanco.

1407
01:23:27,760 --> 01:23:31,720
<i>Years later, the police</i>
<i>managed to place an undercover officer</i>

1408
01:23:31,800 --> 01:23:33,520
<i>inside the new Donosti commando.</i>

1409
01:23:34,040 --> 01:23:37,160
<i>She brought down</i>
<i>the two terrorists in that unit,</i>

1410
01:23:37,240 --> 01:23:41,360
<i>and one of them eventually admitted</i>
<i>that Txapote had personally told him</i>

1411
01:23:41,440 --> 01:23:45,760
<i>about Miguel's execution and that</i>
<i>there had even been doubts within the unit</i>

1412
01:23:45,840 --> 01:23:47,720
<i>about whether to kill him.</i>

1413
01:23:47,800 --> 01:23:49,800
<i>But Txapote was sure.</i>

1414
01:23:51,440 --> 01:23:55,600
When that armed group was dismantled,

1415
01:23:56,200 --> 01:23:57,680
we learned

1416
01:23:58,360 --> 01:24:02,680
that Txapote was the one
who shot Miguel Ángel Blanco twice.

1417
01:24:03,760 --> 01:24:06,040
We know that the other two members
of the commando

1418
01:24:06,120 --> 01:24:08,760
refused to carry out the crime,

1419
01:24:10,160 --> 01:24:13,560
and we also know that Txapote argued

1420
01:24:13,640 --> 01:24:17,040
that the orders of the <i>erakunde</i>,
the organization, must be obeyed.

1421
01:24:17,120 --> 01:24:20,040
Txapote was and is a madman.

1422
01:24:20,720 --> 01:24:22,720
A madman because of what he did.

1423
01:24:23,440 --> 01:24:27,720
A madman because he would
never have backed down.

1424
01:24:28,800 --> 01:24:30,920
Not out of conscience or humanity.

1425
01:24:31,000 --> 01:24:35,320
Txapote was one of the most
important leaders in ETA's history.

1426
01:24:35,920 --> 01:24:38,920
A tremendously charismatic individual,

1427
01:24:39,000 --> 01:24:42,200
admired and respected
by his fellow militants,

1428
01:24:43,520 --> 01:24:46,040
and also one of the most notorious killers

1429
01:24:46,120 --> 01:24:47,840
the organization ever had.

1430
01:24:47,920 --> 01:24:52,480
A madman. He proved it months later when,
as an ETA leader in France,

1431
01:24:52,560 --> 01:24:58,840
he demanded
that his commandos deliver bodies.

1432
01:24:59,960 --> 01:25:05,280
{\an8}In fact, many of the militants
who served under him

1433
01:25:06,520 --> 01:25:08,160
referred to him as "God."

1434
01:25:09,680 --> 01:25:10,920
A madman.

1435
01:25:12,040 --> 01:25:12,880
A madman.

1436
01:25:20,080 --> 01:25:22,160
So many letters arrived.

1437
01:25:22,240 --> 01:25:26,240
Thousands and thousands
and thousands of letters, telegrams,

1438
01:25:26,760 --> 01:25:32,000
drawings people had made of my brother…

1439
01:25:32,080 --> 01:25:36,000
The solidarity of all Spaniards
was tremendous, incredible.

1440
01:25:36,080 --> 01:25:37,720
So many letters from children.

1441
01:25:37,800 --> 01:25:41,720
Many letters from children
who are adults now.

1442
01:25:41,800 --> 01:25:44,520
{\an8}I'M VERY SORRY FOR THE DEATH OF MIGUEL

1443
01:25:44,600 --> 01:25:45,880
I'M SORRY, MIGUEL ÁNGEL
AGE 29!

1444
01:25:45,960 --> 01:25:49,360
And what struck me
was that the address simply read:

1445
01:25:49,440 --> 01:25:51,800
"Miguel Ángel Blanco's family."

1446
01:25:51,880 --> 01:25:54,400
There was no address or anything,

1447
01:25:54,480 --> 01:25:55,960
and the letters still arrived.

1448
01:25:56,040 --> 01:25:58,680
NO TO ETA

1449
01:25:58,760 --> 01:26:02,560
Reading them was deeply painful.
They were comforting, but they hurt.

1450
01:26:05,480 --> 01:26:09,440
{\an8}MIGUEL, SPAIN LOVES YOU
MIGUEL IS WATCHING OVER US

1451
01:26:09,520 --> 01:26:14,880
{\an8}Victims of terrorism must remain
at the heart of the message

1452
01:26:15,400 --> 01:26:17,440
and of our collective memory.

1453
01:26:18,280 --> 01:26:21,200
Because there are generations
who did not live through terrorism,

1454
01:26:21,280 --> 01:26:25,880
who are practically adults now,
or close to it,

1455
01:26:26,760 --> 01:26:31,800
and they need to know
that this democratic coexistence

1456
01:26:33,280 --> 01:26:37,120
and the life we share today

1457
01:26:37,200 --> 01:26:41,440
came at a tremendous cost:

1458
01:26:41,520 --> 01:26:44,720
suffering, pain, and lives lost.

1459
01:26:44,800 --> 01:26:46,800
And it is certainly

1460
01:26:48,120 --> 01:26:53,360
surprising and, in some way, intolerable

1461
01:26:53,440 --> 01:26:56,680
that there are still people

1462
01:26:57,360 --> 01:27:00,840
who cheer on
those responsible for all that pain.

1463
01:27:00,920 --> 01:27:02,880
The state could not
have done anything else.

1464
01:27:02,960 --> 01:27:07,280
It could not
give up defending the rule of law.

1465
01:27:07,360 --> 01:27:10,400
It could not negotiate
with a terrorist organization.

1466
01:27:11,320 --> 01:27:13,480
What if you had been the one kidnapped?

1467
01:27:14,320 --> 01:27:18,600
Well, I think I would have
thought the same thing.

1468
01:27:19,120 --> 01:27:21,840
From minute one, I would've thought,
"They're gonna kill me."

1469
01:27:22,600 --> 01:27:26,560
I always believed
you could not bow to that demand.

1470
01:27:26,640 --> 01:27:31,320
Obviously, you try to save your own life.

1471
01:27:31,400 --> 01:27:33,040
I would probably have thought,

1472
01:27:33,120 --> 01:27:35,600
"Those bastards
are going to let them kill me."

1473
01:27:35,680 --> 01:27:41,600
I insist, I am convinced
my brother would have fully agreed

1474
01:27:41,680 --> 01:27:44,240
that a government
cannot give in to ETA's blackmail.

1475
01:27:44,320 --> 01:27:48,080
From the way he thought
and what he used to say at home,

1476
01:27:49,320 --> 01:27:50,360
I am sure of that.

1477
01:27:50,440 --> 01:27:54,320
It was unthinkable
that the government could bow

1478
01:27:54,840 --> 01:27:58,440
to a request like that,
to a demand like that,

1479
01:27:58,520 --> 01:28:01,040
because if they accepted that one,

1480
01:28:01,560 --> 01:28:04,160
what would be next?
Euskadi's independence?

1481
01:28:04,240 --> 01:28:09,440
It is hard to talk
about the Blanco family without emotion.

1482
01:28:09,520 --> 01:28:13,480
They understood everything
that had to be done,

1483
01:28:13,560 --> 01:28:16,440
they understood
what the government was doing.

1484
01:28:17,200 --> 01:28:21,160
I think I would have had a crisis
of conscience if I had done the opposite

1485
01:28:21,240 --> 01:28:23,440
of what I did on that day.

1486
01:28:24,200 --> 01:28:26,080
I have no doubt.

1487
01:28:27,080 --> 01:28:31,640
Miguel Ángel Blanco's murder
is not the government's responsibility.

1488
01:28:32,160 --> 01:28:33,920
The murderers
are responsible for the murder.

1489
01:28:34,000 --> 01:28:38,360
I was once told that
if they had kidnapped my son,

1490
01:28:39,280 --> 01:28:41,800
I would have behaved differently.

1491
01:28:43,200 --> 01:28:47,040
That if it had been Aznar's son,
we would have done something else.

1492
01:28:47,120 --> 01:28:50,800
You have to be…

1493
01:28:51,800 --> 01:28:55,480
morally bankrupt

1494
01:28:56,520 --> 01:28:57,800
to say such a thing.

1495
01:28:57,880 --> 01:29:00,200
If your son is kidnapped,

1496
01:29:00,280 --> 01:29:04,480
and at that moment
you want to negotiate or talk,

1497
01:29:04,560 --> 01:29:07,040
the first thing you have to do
is leave the government.

1498
01:29:07,120 --> 01:29:08,800
My mother once said:

1499
01:29:08,880 --> 01:29:13,440
"If my son's death
helped bring an end to ETA,

1500
01:29:13,520 --> 01:29:14,880
then so be it."

1501
01:29:14,960 --> 01:29:17,000
People would tell me
it was the beginning of the end,

1502
01:29:17,080 --> 01:29:18,720
and I'd ask, "What beginning of the end?"

1503
01:29:18,800 --> 01:29:22,520
All I felt
was that I went up those stairs,

1504
01:29:22,600 --> 01:29:24,040
walked into my house,

1505
01:29:24,120 --> 01:29:28,080
and my brother's chair was empty,
his room was empty.

1506
01:29:28,160 --> 01:29:32,320
I could see nothing beyond the fact
that I had lost my brother.

1507
01:29:32,920 --> 01:29:38,440
But over time,
I realized that phrase was true,

1508
01:29:38,520 --> 01:29:42,760
as the pain gradually began
to let me see a small ray of light.

1509
01:29:42,840 --> 01:29:46,320
No words could honor a mother more.

1510
01:29:53,920 --> 01:29:57,160
<i>Miguel's grave was constantly vandalized.</i>

1511
01:29:57,240 --> 01:29:59,920
<i>Graffiti,</i>
<i>the glass over his photo smashed,</i>

1512
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:01,600
<i>his flowers stolen…</i>

1513
01:30:02,200 --> 01:30:05,280
<i>ETA's sympathizers</i>
<i>became so morally degraded</i>

1514
01:30:05,360 --> 01:30:08,800
<i>that destroying a grave</i>
<i>seemed like an achievement to them.</i>

1515
01:30:09,440 --> 01:30:11,960
<i>Eventually, the family</i>
<i>moved his remains to Galicia.</i>

1516
01:30:12,680 --> 01:30:15,480
<i>Now he rests</i>
<i>in a small cemetery in Faramontaos,</i>

1517
01:30:15,560 --> 01:30:18,400
<i>a tiny village near Ourense.</i>

1518
01:30:20,120 --> 01:30:22,200
<i>There is something moving</i>
<i>about being here.</i>

1519
01:30:22,720 --> 01:30:26,520
<i>A feeling of respect, but also of sadness.</i>

1520
01:30:27,680 --> 01:30:29,600
<i>You would be my age now, Miguel.</i>

1521
01:30:30,200 --> 01:30:31,480
<i>Maybe with a family.</i>

1522
01:30:32,000 --> 01:30:35,120
<i>Maybe you would even</i>
<i>still be playing drums with your friends.</i>

1523
01:30:36,080 --> 01:30:38,600
<i>You missed seeing Messi</i>
<i>play for your beloved Barça</i>

1524
01:30:38,680 --> 01:30:40,960
<i>and Bunbury's solo career.</i>

1525
01:30:42,160 --> 01:30:44,960
<i>"What was all that for?" you may wonder.</i>

1526
01:30:46,200 --> 01:30:49,240
{\an8}<i>You should know</i>
<i>that your murder made us lose our fear,</i>

1527
01:30:50,000 --> 01:30:53,440
{\an8}<i>that those 48 hours changed everything.</i>

1528
01:30:54,440 --> 01:30:55,800
{\an8}<i>That your death,</i>

1529
01:30:55,880 --> 01:30:58,160
{\an8}<i>and it kills me to say it like this,</i>

1530
01:30:58,240 --> 01:31:00,520
{\an8}<i>was the beginning of the end of ETA.</i>

1531
01:31:00,600 --> 01:31:02,880
{\an8}JULY 13, 1997, AGE 29

1532
01:31:02,960 --> 01:31:04,320
{\an8}<i>I'll tell you something else.</i>

1533
01:31:04,400 --> 01:31:06,480
{\an8}<i>Sixty percent of the young people</i>
<i>in this country</i>

1534
01:31:06,560 --> 01:31:08,800
{\an8}<i>who made it to the end of the documentary</i>

1535
01:31:08,880 --> 01:31:10,480
{\an8}<i>did not know who you were.</i>

1536
01:31:10,560 --> 01:31:12,440
<i>I suppose it is not their fault,</i>

1537
01:31:12,520 --> 01:31:15,160
<i>but ours, because we never told them.</i>

1538
01:31:15,680 --> 01:31:18,760
<i>That is where we are, Miguel.</i>
<i>Rest in peace.</i>

1539
01:31:19,280 --> 01:31:21,360
The beginning of the end of ETA
took 15 years.

1540
01:31:21,440 --> 01:31:23,520
67 people were murdered during that time.

1541
01:31:23,600 --> 01:31:27,680
The terrorist group,
defeated by the police and by society,

1542
01:31:27,760 --> 01:31:29,640
laid down its arms in 2011.

1543
01:31:30,440 --> 01:31:33,680
The social mobilization of those days
gave rise to the spirit of Ermua.

1544
01:31:33,760 --> 01:31:36,160
Streets and squares across Spain
honor Miguel Ángel.

1545
01:31:36,240 --> 01:31:39,720
{\an8}In Ermua, a new sports center
was named in his honor.

1546
01:31:39,800 --> 01:31:42,000
{\an8}MIGUEL ÁNGEL BLANCO SPORTS CENTER

1547
01:31:42,080 --> 01:31:45,000
Those 48 hours
remain etched in everyone's memory.

1548
01:31:46,960 --> 01:31:50,840
<i>We were at the amusement park.</i>
<i>It was summer, we were on vacation,</i>

1549
01:31:50,920 --> 01:31:54,080
<i>and suddenly, everything stopped</i>
<i>and we held a minute of silence.</i>

1550
01:31:54,160 --> 01:31:55,440
IN MEMORY OF MIGUEL

1551
01:31:55,520 --> 01:31:58,400
<i>People were talking about it on the bus.</i>
<i>Word was spreading.</i>

1552
01:31:58,480 --> 01:32:01,320
<i>We were gathered as a family,</i>
<i>listening to the news.</i>

1553
01:32:01,400 --> 01:32:03,080
<i>We took to the streets.</i>

1554
01:32:03,160 --> 01:32:05,920
<i>That night, I could not fall asleep.</i>

1555
01:32:06,000 --> 01:32:08,360
<i>It felt like everything suddenly stopped.</i>

1556
01:32:08,440 --> 01:32:11,240
<i>After lunch,</i>
<i>everything came to a sudden halt.</i>

1557
01:32:11,760 --> 01:32:14,560
<i>I remember it as if it were yesterday.</i>

1558
01:32:14,640 --> 01:32:17,880
<i>In the bar's kitchen,</i>
<i>we spent those days glued to the radio.</i>

1559
01:32:17,960 --> 01:32:20,240
<i>I can still see my mother's outrage</i>
<i>when she heard the news.</i>

1560
01:32:20,320 --> 01:32:22,720
<i>I remember thinking</i>
<i>they would not dare kill him.</i>

1561
01:32:22,800 --> 01:32:24,560
<i>I remember seeing his picture on TV.</i>

1562
01:32:24,640 --> 01:32:27,440
<i>It was like looking in the mirror.</i>
<i>He was a young man like me.</i>

1563
01:32:27,520 --> 01:32:30,760
<i>I was watching my children play</i>
<i>while thinking,</i>

1564
01:32:31,280 --> 01:32:33,400
<i>"Enough is enough!"</i>

1565
01:32:51,520 --> 01:32:52,400
ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

1566
01:32:52,480 --> 01:32:54,280
HIS MAJESTY THE KING
MARÍA DEL MAR BLANCO

1567
01:32:54,360 --> 01:32:56,000
EVERYONE WHO SHARED THEIR TESTIMONY

1568
01:33:07,120 --> 01:33:10,200
THANK YOU TO OUR FAMILIES
FOR TEACHING US TO LIVE IN A DEMOCRACY

1569
01:33:10,960 --> 01:33:14,040
THANK YOU TO EVERYONE
WHO MADE THIS DOCUMENTARY POSSIBLE

1570
01:33:14,120 --> 01:33:17,440
IN MEMORIAM:
MARÍA JOSÉ GURRUTXAGA AND FERNANDO ÓNEGA

1571
01:33:17,520 --> 01:33:20,440
NO ANIMALS WERE HARMED
IN THE MAKING OF THIS DOCUMENTARY

1572
01:33:25,160 --> 01:33:27,520
Subtitle translation by: Felipe H. Mathews